// Runtime/SlabRuntime.maxon — ⭐⭐ **THE OBJECT LAYER: SIZE CLASSES, SPANS, AND THE HYBRID FREE LIST.**
// `SlabArena.maxon` beneath this file answers *"where does an 8 KiB chunk come from?"*; this file answers
// *"which slot of which span serves a 37-byte request, and where does it go when it dies?"*. Together they
// replace the bump allocator `MmRuntime.buildSlabAlloc` used to be — a cursor that never reclaimed a byte.
//
// It is v1's `stdlib/Internals.maxon:109-1348` re-derived against shv2's tiers. What is TAKEN is recorded
// against each item; what is NOT taken is recorded too, because every one of those is a decision.
//
// ⭐⭐ **S5 TOOK THE THREE ITEMS THIS HEADER USED TO LIST AS "NOT TAKEN" — the global lock, the per-P
// remote-free MPSC queues and the `owning_p` gate — BECAUSE G1 BUILT REAL Ps AND THE SLAB WAS MEASURED
// BREAKING UNDER THEM.** `maxon-shv2/track0/alloc-torture.maxon` at `MAXON_MAX_PROCS=2` died with **exit 86,
// `slabSpanExhaustedPastItsEnd`** — INV-1 below, tripped because two OS threads were popping slots out of
// ONE unsharded mcache row. At 7 it segfaulted outright. The allocator naming its own invariant is what
// localised that to this file rather than leaving a mystery fault in the scheduler.
//
// **THE SHAPE, IN FOUR SENTENCES.** Each P owns its own mcache ROW (`emitSlabCurrentP` answers the P, the row
// is its clamped `id`) and is the SOLE WRITER of every span cached there, so the pop is lockless. A span
// carries the P that owns it (`MspanOwningPOffset`), and a cached span whose owner is not me is a MISS — not
// something to pop — which is the one check a `free_count` test structurally cannot make. A free by a P that
// does not own the slot's span CAS-pushes it onto the owner's Treiber queue at `POffRemoteFreeHead`, and the
// owner replays the whole chain on its next alloc slow path. Everything that touches state more than one P
// can reach — refill, the mcentral lists, the metadata slab, the arena, the scavenger — runs under ONE
// recursive lock in the state region; the fast path never takes it.
//
// ⚠ **ALL OF IT IS A BUILD-TIME ARGUMENT (`sharded`), AND THAT IS NOT A MICRO-OPTIMISATION.** `sharded` is
// `usage.usesGt`: a program with no green threads has no P, no second OS thread and therefore nothing to
// guard, and `osLockEnter`/`tlsSlotLoad` do not even LOWER on the wasm and arm64 lanes (`StdToWasm` refuses
// both at emission; `StdToArm64Conversion` has no case for either). So the choice is not "is the lock worth
// its cycles" but "does this program have a scheduler at all" — and the same bit already decides whether
// `installGtRuntime` runs. `zeroed` and `countRaw` below are the same idiom.
//
// ⚠ **THE TWO-EPOCH SCAVENGER WAS ON THAT "NOT TAKEN" LIST AND S6 TOOK IT — but only the GUARD, not the
// mechanism it guards.** v1 decommits a SPAN's own footprint and keeps the span; shv2 destroys the span,
// gives its chunks back to the arena, and decommits at the arena's 64 KiB commit granule. The reason is not
// taste: a span's footprint is 8 KiB-granular, `madvise` refuses an address that is not page-aligned, and
// arm64-macOS's page is 16 KiB — see `SlabArena.buildSlabArenaScavenge` for the two ways v1's shape goes
// wrong there and for why Go answers the same question at the page layer too.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE CENTREPIECE: A SPAN CARRIES BOTH A FREE LIST AND A VIRGIN BUMP REGION.**
// A slot reaches a caller from one of two places, and they differ in whether the memory is DIRTY:
//
//   * the FREE LIST — a RECYCLED slot. It holds the previous occupant's bytes plus the link written into
//     its first word when it was freed, so it MUST be zeroed before the caller sees it.
//   * the BUMP REGION `[bump_next, bump_end)` — slots that have NEVER been handed out. Their pages came
//     straight from `__slab_arena_alloc_chunks`, i.e. fresh `VirtualAlloc`/`mmap` address space, which
//     every target shv2 emits guarantees arrives ZEROED. They cost NOTHING to hand out.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THIS IS WHY THERE IS NO `__slab_build_freelist`, AND v1 DELETED ITS OWN.** Threading an intrusive
// free list through a fresh span writes a next-pointer into slot[0] of EVERY slot, which DIRTIES every one
// of them — so building the list up front was not merely wasted work, it was the thing that would FORCE a
// memzero on every first-ever allocation. **Leaving the region pristine is the precondition for any
// zero-elision at all.** It is FINER-GRAINED than Go, whose `needzero` is per-SPAN: Go re-zeroes even
// never-used slots in a span that has seen a single free, while this cursor is per-SLOT.
//
// **THE THREE INVARIANTS. Every mutation site below re-establishes them:**
//   INV-1  free_count == |free_list| + (bump_end - bump_next) / slot_size
//   INV-2  bump_end   == base_addr + slot_size * total_slots   (DERIVED, never stored)
//   INV-3  every byte in [bump_next, bump_end) is ZERO
//
// **INV-1 TRAPS** (`RuntimeAbort.slabSpanExhaustedPastItsEnd`): a `free_count > 0` with an empty free list
// and an exhausted bump region would hand out a slot PAST the span's end, silently corrupting whatever the
// arena placed in the next chunk. v1 traps at the same point and for the same reason.
//
// ⭐ The case that looks wrong and is not: a span may be returned to mcentral carrying BOTH an unconsumed
// bump region AND a populated free list. (Take 3 slots from a fresh 1024-slot span, free all 3 →
// `free_count == 1024 == total_slots` → returned, holding 3 free-list entries and 1021 never-used slots.)
// Both survive in the mspan and the next taker keeps using both; INV-1 holds throughout.
//
// ⭐ **WHO MAY WRITE WHAT — the whole concurrency argument, in one table, because every `if` below is an
// instance of it.** ⚠ It is about a SHARDED build; an unsharded one has one thread and every row reads
// "anyone".
//
//   | state                                   | writer                                   | protection      |
//   |-----------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|-----------------|
//   | a span's free list / free_count         | the P in its `owning_p`, ONLY            | sole ownership  |
//   | `mcache[row][class]`                    | the P whose id clamps to `row`           | sole ownership  |
//   | a P's remote-free queue head            | any P (push) / the owner (detach)        | `atomicCas`     |
//   | mcentral heads, metadata slab, arena,   | anyone                                   | `__slab_lock`   |
//   |   the arena map's LEVELS, os-direct     |                                          |                 |
//   | the arena map's ENTRIES (read side)     | —                                        | aligned words   |
//
// ⚠ **THE RAW ROW BREAKS THE SECOND ROW OF THAT TABLE AND IS WHY THE LOCK REACHES THE FAST PATH AT ALL.**
// `SlabRawShardRow` is shared by every OS thread that owns no P, so *"the P whose id clamps to this row"* is
// not one thread there. Both ends therefore serialise: a class-alloc whose row IS the raw row takes the lock
// (`buildSlabAllocEntry`), and a free of a span whose owner is `MspanOwningPNone` takes it too
// (`buildSlabFree`) — but only once a scheduler exists, because before that the lone main thread is the only
// writer and would be paying for a race it cannot have.

// A machine word, for the COMPILE-TIME size arithmetic below — the mspan record's width, the mcache row's
// stride, and where the state region's second array starts. The EMITTED `base + index · 8` is not spelled
// here at all: it is `MmRuntime.emitWordSlotAddr`, exported at S2 precisely so a third copy of that
// arithmetic did not get written. What stays private is the LAYOUT constant, because a shared "8" is not a
// shared fact — these are strides of tables this file owns.
let WordBytes = 8

// --- The mspan record ---
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE CLASS IS STORED ONCE. v1 STORES IT THREE TIMES.** Its mspan carries `slot_size` (+0x08),
// `class_idx` (+0x28) AND `total_slots` (+0x30), with `slot_size == __slab_class_size(class_idx)` and
// `total_slots == __slab_objs_per_span(class_idx)` — two derived facts written down beside the fact they
// derive from, held together by a comment (*"kept in lock-step with"*). Here only `class_idx` is stored and
// the geometry is read from the `.rdata` table below.
//
// ⚠ **THAT IS A TRADE TO MEASURE, NOT A WIN.** The hot path used to reach `slot_size` with ONE load from a
// record it already holds; it now costs one load of `class_idx` plus one indexed `.rdata` load — and the
// `.rdata` line is shared by every span of every class, so it is the same cache line for the whole program
// where the per-span copies were not. Which of those dominates is a measurement nobody here has taken; what
// IS certain is that two numbers that cannot disagree have replaced three that could.
let MspanBaseAddrOffset = 0 as ByteOffset
let MspanFreeListOffset = 8 as ByteOffset
let MspanFreeCountOffset = 16 as ByteOffset
let MspanNextSpanOffset = 24 as ByteOffset
let MspanClassIdxOffset = 32 as ByteOffset
let MspanBumpNextOffset = 40 as ByteOffset

// ⭐⭐ **WHO OWNS THIS SPAN — the P pointer, `MspanOwningPNone`, or `MspanOwningPParked`.** It answers two
// questions no other field can, and both of them are corruption when guessed:
//
//   * **MAY I POP FROM IT?** A span recycled to another P keeps `free_count != 0`, so a depletion test
//     cannot tell a span this row still owns from one it merely still points at. v1 fixed this twice, in
//     both of its backends, and its own note calls the depletion check *"unable to detect a RECYCLED span"*.
//   * **WHERE DOES A FREE OF ONE OF ITS SLOTS GO?** To this P's own free list if it is mine, and onto the
//     OWNER's remote queue otherwise — which is the whole of `buildSlabFree`'s routing.
//
// ⭐ **IT IS A P POINTER WHERE v1 STORES A P *ID*, AND THE POINTER IS THE BETTER OF THE TWO.** v1's remote
// push must turn its id back into a P (`__slab_proc_at` → `__sched_procs[id]`), which costs two loads on
// every cross-P free and made its own sentinel path read `__sched_procs[0xFFFFFFFF]` — a wild load 32 GiB
// past the array, unreachable only by luck. A pointer needs no array, no bound and no second export from
// `SchedRuntime`. The one thing the id buys — the mcache ROW — is one load away (`POffId`), on the alloc
// path that needs the row anyway.
//
// ⭐ **AND IT REPLACES the `cached_shard` word rather than joining it, so the record stays 64 bytes.** That
// word existed to spare `__slab_span_return` v1's 256-row search for the row still pointing at this span;
// the owner's row answers the same question in the same O(1), because a span can only ever be cached in its
// OWNER's row — `__slab_refill` installs it and stamps the owner in one block, and it reaches mcentral only
// through the return below, which clears that row first.
let MspanOwningPOffset = 48 as ByteOffset

// ⭐⭐ **THE TWO-EPOCH GRACE GUARD (S6) — ONE WORD, AND IT IS WHAT KEEPS AN ACTIVE WORKING SET RESIDENT.**
// The scavenger only destroys a span it has already SEEN idle on an mcentral list once before, so a span
// that empties and refills between two calls never reaches the release path and pays neither the chunk-free
// memzero nor a syscall. Without it a workload that cycles a population pays both, per cycle, forever.
// v1 carries the identical guard at its own `+0x48` and records the same reason.
let MspanScavStateOffset = 56 as ByteOffset

// ⭐ **THE STATES, AND WHY THERE ARE TWO WHERE v1 HAS THREE.** v1's third is `DECOMMITTED`: it drops a span's
// pages and KEEPS the span, so a later reuse has to re-back it (`__slab_reback_span`). shv2 releases the
// span's CHUNKS instead — to the arena, where any class can take them and where whole granules become
// decommittable — and a span with no chunks is not a state, it is gone. So the machine is
// `ACTIVE → SEEN_FREE → destroyed`, and the terminal transition has no value to store because it has no
// record to store one in.
//
// ⚠ **`ACTIVE` IS 0, WHICH IS NOT AN ACCIDENT AND IS NOT LOAD-BEARING EITHER.** `__slab_refill` writes it
// explicitly on both paths (see the `install` block), because a recycled mspan header holds STALE DATA
// rather than zero — the rule `buildSlabMetaAlloc` states in full.
let MspanScavActive = 0
let MspanScavSeenFree = 1

// ⭐ **THE RECORD'S WIDTH, DERIVED FROM ITS LAST FIELD — and it is stated here because the check that reads
// it had ALREADY gone stale.** `checkSlabRuntimeGeometry` computed the record as
// `MspanCachedShardOffset + WordBytes`, which was the last field when it was written and stopped being one
// the moment S6 appended `MspanScavStateOffset` a word past it: the guard against overlapping headers was
// silently measuring 56 bytes of a 64-byte record. Naming the width once, beside the field that determines
// it, is what makes the next appended field move it.
let MspanRecordBytes = (MspanScavStateOffset as ParsedInt) + WordBytes

// One mspan header's slot in the metadata slab. The record occupies 0x00..0x40 exactly, and 64 keeps
// successive headers 16-byte aligned inside the 8 KiB metadata chunk. `checkSlabRuntimeGeometry` asserts the
// record fits rather than trusting this number — v1's own comment warns that a stale value here silently
// OVERLAPS adjacent mspan headers, and the record is full, so the next field to be added is the one that has
// to move this constant.
let MspanSlotBytes = 64

// ⭐ **THE TWO NON-POINTER VALUES `owning_p` TAKES, and they are different facts rather than two spellings
// of "nobody".** `None` is a REAL owner — the shared raw row, whose spans belong to whichever P-less OS
// thread reaches them and whose accesses serialise under the lock. `Parked` means the span is on an mcentral
// list, owned by no one and holding no live slot; a free that observes it is a double free or a slot that
// outlived its span, which is why `buildSlabFree` aborts on it rather than picking an arm.
//
// ⚠ v1 spells `Parked` `0xFFFFFFFF` and REACHES IT WITH A WILD LOAD (see `MspanOwningPOffset`). `-1` cannot
// be a P pointer, cannot be `None`, and needs no array lookup to be recognised.
let MspanOwningPNone = 0
let MspanOwningPParked = -1

// --- Sharding ---
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE mcache WAS BUILT AT FULL SHARD WIDTH BEFORE ANY ROW BUT ONE WAS USED, AND S5 IS THE RUNG THAT
// COLLECTED ON IT.** The STRUCTURE was real from S2 — 256 rows of one span pointer per class — while the
// SELECTOR was degenerate, so turning the selector on is a change to FUNCTION BODIES and not to the layout
// every address in this file is computed from. The rows cost 256 × 68 × 8 = 139,264 bytes of an arena that
// is committed in whole 2 MiB units either way (`SlabArena.maxon`), so the width is paid for out of chunks
// the process already owns.
let SlabMaxShards = 256

// ⭐⭐ **ROW 255 IS THE RAW-THREAD ROW, AND A NEGATIVE ID MAPS TO IT — NOT TO 0.** v1 records the measured
// consequence of the other choice: a raw OS thread sharing row 0 with P[0] races that P's LOCKLESS pop and
// hands one slab slot to both, which is heap corruption with no diagnostic. The reservation costs one row
// in 256 and it is what makes "no live P" a state of its own rather than an alias for the first one.
let SlabRawShardRow = SlabMaxShards - 1

// --- The `.rdata` tables ---
//
// ⭐⭐ **ONE PACKED WORD PER CLASS: `slot_size | (objects_per_span << 32)`.** Both numbers are pure
// functions of the class index (`SlabClassTable`), and the hot path wants `slot_size` on every allocation
// while `objects_per_span` is wanted only by INV-1's guard and by the fully-free test. Packing them into
// ONE 8-byte entry makes the span geometry ONE indexed load instead of two, which is the whole of what the
// "store `class_idx` alone" decision costs.
//
// ⚠ **THE SHIFT IS 32 AND NOT THE 16 THE WIDTHS WOULD ALLOW.** A slot size needs 16 bits (32768) and an
// object count 11 (1024), so both would fit either side of a 16-bit split — and then the day a class ladder
// grows past 65535 the two fields would silently overlap. At 32 the fields have 4× the headroom their
// domains need and the extraction masks read as what they are.
let SlabClassGeomSlotShift = 32
let SlabClassGeomSlotMask = 0xFFFFFFFF

let SlabSizeToClass8Label = "__slab_size_to_class8"
let SlabSizeToClass128Label = "__slab_size_to_class128"
let SlabClassGeomLabel = "__slab_class_geom"

// ⭐ **THE TWO REVERSE LOOKUPS' DIVISORS, AS SHIFTS.** `SlabClasses.maxon` states the divisors
// (`SlabSmallSizeDiv` 8, `SlabLargeSizeDiv` 128); the emitted code divides by them, and a divide by a power
// of two is a shift. A width and the shift that divides by it are ONE fact written twice by necessity, so
// `checkSlabRuntimeGeometry` asserts the pair agrees — the rule `SlabArena.requireShiftMatchesWidth` states
// in full for its own four pairs.
let SlabSmallSizeDivShift = 3
let SlabLargeSizeDivShift = 7

// ⭐ **THE SMALL TABLE'S UPPER BOUND, WHICH IS GO'S AND IS NOT `SlabSmallSizeMax`.** Go routes
// `size <= smallSizeMax - smallSizeDiv` (1016) through the 8-stepped table and everything above it through
// the 128-stepped one, because index `(1024+7)/8 == 128` is the 8-table's LAST entry and the 128-table's
// entry 0 already answers for 1017..1024. Written as the derivation rather than as 1016 so a changed
// divisor moves it.
let SlabSmallTableMaxSize = SlabSmallSizeMax - SlabSmallSizeDiv

// --- The one `.data` word this family owns ---
//
// ⭐ **ALL OF THE ALLOCATOR'S MUTABLE STATE LIVES BEHIND ONE POINTER.** `.data` slots are laid out into
// every heap program's image and are rendered into every golden fragment, so each one is a cost paid by
// every program that allocates a single box. The mcentral heads, the mcache rows and the metadata slab's
// bump cursor are all ARRAYS or PAIRS — none of them can live in a `.data` word anyway (`layOut` orders
// single words in an order this table may not depend on) — so they share one lazily-committed region and
// this word is its base. Zero means "not yet committed", which is the state the `.data` image already
// expresses; there is nothing to initialise and so nothing to guard.
let SlabStateLabel = b"__slab_state"

// The region's fixed head, then the two arrays. `SlabStateMcentralOffset` onwards are DERIVED from the
// class count so a regenerated ladder moves them rather than leaving a stale stride behind.
//
// ⭐ **`metaFree` IS S6's, AND IT EXISTS BECAUSE SOMETHING FINALLY DESTROYS SPANS.** `buildSlabMetaAlloc`'s
// note used to say a free list here would be "a structure with no producer"; the scavenger is that producer,
// and without it every span it destroys would leak its 64-byte header for the life of the process.
let SlabStateMetaBumpOffset = 0 as ByteOffset
let SlabStateMetaEndOffset = 8 as ByteOffset
let SlabStateMetaFreeOffset = 16 as ByteOffset

// ⭐⭐ **THE GLOBAL SLOW-PATH LOCK LIVES *HERE*, IN THE STATE REGION, AND NOT IN A `.data` WORD — WHICH IS A
// GOLDEN-FRAGMENT ARGUMENT, NOT A TIDINESS ONE.** Every committed fragment under `specs-shv2/fragments/`
// renders the whole `data { … }` table with each label's byte OFFSET, so one new `.data` word shifts every
// label after it and re-mints **every heap program's golden in the suite**. A CRITICAL_SECTION reached
// through the pointer `__slab_state` already holds costs the image nothing and the fragments nothing.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS INITIALISED INSIDE `__slab_state_base`'s COMMIT ARM, WHICH IS THE ONE PLACE THAT PROVABLY RUNS
// BEFORE A SECOND THREAD EXISTS.** `__gt_init` allocates the scheduler's own lock and its P array through
// `__slab_alloc` before `__sched_init_procs` publishes a TLS slot, so the region — and this lock — are
// committed by the main thread while it is still the only one. That is the same argument v1 makes for its
// explicit `__slab_init()`, made from the lazy commit shv2 already had rather than from a new entry point.
//
// `OsLockBytes` are RESERVED on every lane, sharded or not, so the layout — and `checkSlabRuntimeGeometry`'s
// arithmetic over it — is one thing rather than two. ⚠ That constant is the MAXIMUM over the targets this
// compiler serves (64, a Darwin `pthread_mutex_t`) rather than any one target's own lock size, and
// `MmRuntime.OsLockBytes` carries why: making it target-dependent would fork THIS offset, a struct layout
// every allocation in the program is addressed through, to save 24 bytes on one lock per family.
//
// Only the `osLockInit`/`osLockEnter` OPS are build-time gated, and that half has shrunk: wasm refuses them
// still, but arm64-macOS lowers all three (`pthread_mutex_init` with a RECURSIVE attribute — see
// `Arm64DarwinRuntime.buildArm64DarwinLockInitChunk` for why the attribute is load-bearing and how its
// absence was measured, as a hang in this very function's refill path).
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE LOCK ORDER IS `__sched_lock` → `__slab_lock`, AND IT HOLDS BECAUSE THIS SIDE NEVER TAKES THE
// OTHER ONE.** The scheduler allocates while holding its run-queue lock (`__gt_spawn`, `__gt_init`), so that
// nesting is routine; nothing under `__slab_alloc`/`__slab_free` reaches a scheduler entry point, so the
// reverse nesting does not exist and there is no order for two files to remember. **A future caller that
// makes the allocator call into `GtRuntime` while holding this lock introduces a deadlock**, which is why
// the direction is written down here rather than left to be re-derived.
let SlabStateLockOffset = 24 as ByteOffset
let SlabStateMcentralOffset = ((SlabStateLockOffset as ParsedInt) + OsLockBytes) as ByteOffset

// The value the state `.data` word holds until the region is committed. Named because it is READ in two
// places (the probe below) and WRITTEN in one (`buildSlabStateBase`'s publishing store), and a bare 0 at
// three sites is three chances for one of them to stop meaning the same thing as the others.
let SlabStateUncommitted = 0 as ParsedInt

// Whether THIS allocation took `__slab_lock` on its way in, carried across the pop as a block argument so
// that exactly one copy of the pop serves both arms — see `buildSlabAllocEntry`. Named rather than 1/0
// because the value's meaning is *"there is a `Leave` outstanding"*, which a bare literal at the release
// site would not say.
let SlabLockTaken = 1
let SlabLockNotTaken = 0

// --- Entry points ---

// The public allocator door. `MmRuntime` owns the two names because `__mm_alloc` chooses between them (the
// counted one and its uncounted twin), and the choice is that file's to make.

// ⭐ **THE NON-ZEROING DOOR — Go's `mallocgc(…, needzero: false)`.**
//
// ⚠⚠ **AUDIT RULE — read this before adding a caller.** `__slab_alloc_raw` may ONLY be used where the
// caller provably writes EVERY BYTE of the returned region before anything else can read it, AND the region
// is never walked as MANAGED POINTERS. A region is "walked as managed pointers" if an element walk, a
// hash-table teardown or an `array.set` old-occupant decref will ever run over it; such a buffer MUST come
// from `__slab_alloc`. A non-zeroed slot read as a pointer and decref'd is exactly the bug the zeroing
// contract exists to prevent, and v1 records it happening (a sparsely-filled hash table whose unwritten
// slots held the previous occupant's garbage, decref'd as if live).
//
// ⚠ **IT HAS NO CALLER IN shv2 TODAY, AND SO COSTS NOTHING**: `DeadFunctionElimination` runs after every
// `install*Runtime` and drops it from every program. It is built anyway for the reason `SlabArena.maxon`
// built the page layer before anything was wired to it — the door and its audit rule exist before the first
// caller has to invent them, and the ZEROING is a build-time argument (`emitSlabAllocClass`'s `zeroed`), so
// admitting a caller is an argument rather than a body.
let SlabAllocRawName = b"__slab_alloc_raw"
export let SlabFreeName = b"__slab_free"

let SlabRoundedSizeName = b"__slab_rounded_size"
let SlabStateName = b"__slab_state_base"
let SlabMetaAllocName = b"__slab_meta_alloc"
let SlabMetaFreeName = b"__slab_meta_free"
let SlabRefillName = b"__slab_refill"
let SlabFreeToSpanName = b"__slab_free_to_span"
let SlabDrainRemoteName = b"__slab_drain_remote"
let SlabSpanReturnName = b"__slab_span_return"
let SlabSpanDestroyName = b"__slab_span_destroy"
let SlabOsDirectAllocName = b"__slab_os_direct_alloc"
let SlabOsDirectFreeName = b"__slab_os_direct_free"

// ⭐ **THE SCAVENGER'S PUBLIC DOOR.** `__Builtins.scavengeMemory()` lowers straight to it and reads its
// answer — the bytes this call handed back to the OS. Exported because `RuntimeUsage` matches the callee to
// decide whether the program carries the allocator at all: a program whose ONLY heap contact is this call
// still needs every layer under it.
export let SlabScavengeName = b"__slab_scavenge"

// --- The OS-direct tier ---
//
// ⭐⭐ **ABOVE `SlabMaxSmallSize` A REQUEST IS ITS OWN MAPPING, AND ITS SIZE RIDES IN A 16-BYTE PREFIX.**
// A free has to know how many bytes to hand back, and the pointer→span reverse map cannot answer: a map
// MISS *is* the OS-direct sentinel (`SlabArena.ArenaMapNoSpan`), so registering these pages would destroy
// the very signal that identifies them.
//
// ⛔⛔ **v1's ANSWER IS A 512-ENTRY ARRAY WITH A LINEAR SCAN, AND IT IS NOT PORTED — IT HAS A CEILING.**
// `__slab_os_direct_alloc` there exits with sentinel 134 once 512 mappings are live, and
// `__slab_os_direct_free` walks the array comparing pointers. The ceiling is the fatal half: shv2's
// `__mm_alloc` routes every box above 32 KiB here, and a program holding 513 large buffers would abort in
// the ALLOCATOR for no reason of its own. A prefix word has no ceiling, frees in O(1), and costs 16 bytes
// on an allocation that is at least 32 KiB — under 0.05%.
//
// The prefix is 16 bytes rather than 8 so the pointer handed back keeps 16-byte alignment, which is what
// every record layout above this file assumes.
let OsDirectPrefixBytes = 16
let OsDirectTotalBytesOffset = 0 as ByteOffset
let OsDirectMagicOffset = 8 as ByteOffset

// ⭐ **THE GUARD v1 GETS FROM ITS ARRAY SEARCH, RECOVERED IN ONE COMPARE.** Its `__slab_os_direct_free`
// exits 135 when a pointer is not in the tracking array — a double free, or a pointer that was never ours.
// With no array to search there is nothing to miss, so the same fault is caught by a magic word the
// allocator stamps and the free checks. Without it a bogus free reads user data as a byte count and hands
// it to `osFreePages`, which unmaps an arbitrary region.
//
// `"SLABOSD!"` read as a little-endian machine word. Any value would do; a legible one makes a memory dump
// readable, which is the only thing this constant is ever looked at in.
let OsDirectMagic = 0x2144534F42414C53

// --- Installation ---

// Install the object layer. Gated by `usesHeap` at the call site (`Compiler.compileToCodeResult`) exactly
// as `installMmRuntime` is, because this IS the allocator that file's boxes come from.
//
// ⚠ It takes `globalData` because it REGISTERS as well as emits: the three `.rdata` tables must be in the
// table before the backend lays the section out. They are registered here rather than at their reader for
// `installUnicodeCategoryRuntime`'s reason — a `rdataAddr` naming a label nothing registered is a link
// failure, so the register and the emit belong to one call.
export function installSlabRuntime(module StdModule, globalData GlobalDataTable, usage RuntimeUsage)
	if not usage.usesHeap 'noAllocator'
		return
	end 'noAllocator'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE GEOMETRY CHECK SITS BEHIND THE GATE, WHICH IS THE OPPOSITE OF `installSlabArena`'s
	// PLACEMENT — AND THE ARGUMENT THAT PUT IT THERE IS EXACTLY WHAT DOES NOT APPLY HERE.** That check is
	// ahead of its gate because NOTHING set `usesSlabArena` when it was written: behind the gate it would
	// have been a check that never ran, which is this project's own recurring failure wearing a different
	// name. This bit is `usesHeap`, which every program that allocates a single box sets — so the check
	// runs constantly, and running it for the handful that allocate NOTHING buys nothing: a program with no
	// allocator carries no size-class table, no reverse lookup and no span, so no geometry of theirs can be
	// wrong for it.
	//
	// ⚠ It is not free, either. It builds both reverse tables and walks the ladder, which is why it is worth
	// asking who it protects rather than running it at every compile in the suite by reflex.
	checkSlabRuntimeGeometry()

	_ = globalData.registerRdataConstantLabeled(SlabSizeToClass8Label, bytes: slabSizeToClass8Table(), kind: RdataPayloadKind.slabTable)
	_ = globalData.registerRdataConstantLabeled(SlabSizeToClass128Label, bytes: slabSizeToClass128Table(), kind: RdataPayloadKind.slabTable)
	_ = globalData.registerRdataConstantLabeled(SlabClassGeomLabel, bytes: slabClassGeometryTable(), kind: RdataPayloadKind.slabTable)

	// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE BIT THE WHOLE SHARDING HANGS OFF.** See the file header: `usesGt` is not a proxy for
	// "might this be threaded", it IS the question — a program with no green threads has no P to shard by,
	// no second OS thread to race, and (on wasm and arm64) no lowering for the ops the sharding is made of.
	// It is read once, here, so no builder below can ask a differently-worded version of it.
	let sharded = usage.usesGt

	buildSlabStateBase(module, sharded: sharded)
	if sharded 'remoteQueue'
		buildSlabDrainRemote(module)
	end 'remoteQueue'
	buildSlabRoundedSize(module)
	buildSlabMetaAlloc(module)
	buildSlabMetaFree(module)
	buildSlabRefill(module, sharded: sharded)
	buildSlabAllocEntry(module, name: SlabAllocName, countRaw: usage.usesMmCounters, zeroed: true, sharded: sharded)
	buildSlabAllocEntry(module, name: SlabAllocRawName, countRaw: usage.usesMmCounters, zeroed: false, sharded: sharded)
	// The uncounted twin, in a counted build only — the one `__slab_alloc` caller whose bytes are already
	// reported as a TRACKED box (see `MmRuntime.SlabAllocBoxName`).
	if usage.usesMmCounters 'boxTwin'
		buildSlabAllocEntry(module, name: SlabAllocBoxName, countRaw: false, zeroed: true, sharded: sharded)
	end 'boxTwin'
	buildSlabSpanReturn(module, sharded: sharded)
	buildSlabSpanDestroy(module)
	buildSlabScavenge(module, sharded: sharded)
	buildSlabFreeToSpan(module)
	buildSlabFree(module, sharded: sharded)
	buildSlabOsDirectAlloc(module)
	buildSlabOsDirectFree(module)
end 'installSlabRuntime'

// The one `.data` slot the object layer owns. The gate rides inside the producer, following
// `slabArenaRuntimeGlobals` and `SchedRuntime.schedRuntimeGlobals`: a condition spelled at both ends is a
// second place for the two to disagree, and the loop over an empty array is the no-op the absent `if` would
// have produced.
// ⚠ It used to name `clockRuntimeGlobals` first, which W212 DELETED — that file's two words became frame
// slots, so it lays out no `.data` at all and there is no producer left to follow.
export function slabRuntimeGlobals(usage RuntimeUsage) returns DataSectionEntryArray
	var out = DataSectionEntryArray.create()

	if usage.usesHeap 'allocatorState'
		out.push(DataSectionEntry.create(String.from(SlabStateLabel), valueType: StdType.i64, value: 0))
	end 'allocatorState'

	return out
end 'slabRuntimeGlobals'

// ⭐⭐ **THE RECORD LAYOUT IS INTERNALLY COHERENT — asserted, not trusted.** `checkSlabClassTable` and
// `checkSlabArenaGeometry` make the same argument for the ladder and the arena, and state it in full: a
// table whose source changed shape produces a working-LOOKING allocator that indexes past its own bounds.
// It panics rather than returning a verdict, for those checks' reason — a broken layout cannot produce a
// working allocator, so no caller could sensibly continue.
function checkSlabRuntimeGeometry()
	// Reading the ladder here is what makes the derived strides below statements about THIS table rather
	// than about whatever table happened to be generated when they were written.
	checkSlabClassTable()

	if (1 shl SlabSmallSizeDivShift) != SlabSmallSizeDiv 'smallDivShiftDisagrees'
		panic("slab runtime: 1 shl {SlabSmallSizeDivShift} is {1 shl SlabSmallSizeDivShift}, not the small-table divisor {SlabSmallSizeDiv}")
	end 'smallDivShiftDisagrees'

	if (1 shl SlabLargeSizeDivShift) != SlabLargeSizeDiv 'largeDivShiftDisagrees'
		panic("slab runtime: 1 shl {SlabLargeSizeDivShift} is {1 shl SlabLargeSizeDivShift}, not the large-table divisor {SlabLargeSizeDiv}")
	end 'largeDivShiftDisagrees'

	// The mspan record must fit its metadata slot, or successive headers overlap — v1's own warning about
	// the same constant, checked here instead of restated.
	//
	// ⚠ **THIS READ `MspanCachedShardOffset + WordBytes` UNTIL S5, AND THAT HAD BEEN THE WRONG FIELD SINCE
	// S6.** The check was measuring a 56-byte record against a 64-byte slot while the record was already 64:
	// it would have gone on passing for a 72-byte one. `MspanRecordBytes` is stated at the layout, beside
	// the field that determines it, so appending a field moves it.
	if MspanRecordBytes > MspanSlotBytes 'recordOverflowsSlot'
		panic("slab runtime: an mspan record is {MspanRecordBytes} bytes and its metadata slot is {MspanSlotBytes}")
	end 'recordOverflowsSlot'

	// The metadata slab hands slots out of ONE chunk at a time, so a slot larger than a chunk could never be
	// served and the bump would loop forever asking for fresh chunks.
	if MspanSlotBytes > SlabPageBytes 'slotOverflowsChunk'
		panic("slab runtime: an mspan slot is {MspanSlotBytes} bytes and a metadata chunk is {SlabPageBytes}")
	end 'slotOverflowsChunk'

	// The packed geometry word must actually hold both fields. `SlabMaxSmallSize` is the widest slot size
	// and `SlabPageBytes / SlabSmallSizeDiv` bounds the object count (the smallest class in a one-page span).
	if SlabMaxSmallSize > SlabClassGeomSlotMask 'slotSizeOverflowsField'
		panic("slab runtime: a slot size of {SlabMaxSmallSize} does not fit the packed geometry's low field (mask {SlabClassGeomSlotMask})")
	end 'slotSizeOverflowsField'

	// The state region's own arithmetic: the two arrays must not overlap, and the whole must fit the chunk
	// run committed for it.
	if slabStateMcacheOffset() <= SlabStateMcentralOffset 'arraysOverlap'
		panic("slab runtime: the mcache starts at {slabStateMcacheOffset()}, not past the mcentral heads at {SlabStateMcentralOffset}")
	end 'arraysOverlap'

	if slabStateBytes() > slabStateChunks() * SlabPageBytes 'stateOverflowsChunks'
		panic("slab runtime: {slabStateBytes()} bytes of allocator state do not fit {slabStateChunks()} chunk(s) of {SlabPageBytes}")
	end 'stateOverflowsChunks'

	// ⭐⭐ **AND THE TWO REQUESTS THIS FILE MAKES OF THE PAGE LAYER MUST BE SATISFIABLE ON BOTH LANES — the
	// bound that was MISSING until S6.** `slabStateChunks()` is 18 chunks and the non-reserving arena has 63
	// allocatable ones; nothing asserted the pair, so raising `SlabMaxShards` or regenerating a longer class
	// ladder would abort **at run time, on wasm only, in a build whose native twin is perfect**, with
	// `slabChunkRunUnsatisfiable` — a true statement about the arena and a baffling one about the change that
	// caused it. It is asked of BOTH lanes for `checkArenaGeometryForLane`'s stated reason: a rule that only
	// holds for whichever target happens to be compiling is the check that never runs.
	requireArenaCanServe("the allocator's state region", chunks: slabStateChunks())

	// The other request is a SPAN's chunk run, whose largest is the widest class's page count — Go's table
	// tops out at 10. `__slab_refill` computes it as `ceil(slotSize · totalSlots / SlabPageBytes)` at run
	// time; the bound is what says every value that formula can produce is one the arena will serve.
	requireArenaCanServe("the widest class's span", chunks: slabWidestSpanChunks())
end 'checkSlabRuntimeGeometry'

// A chunk run this file will ask `__slab_arena_alloc_chunks` for, checked against what an arena can serve on
// each lane. One helper rather than two pairs of `if`s, so a third request cannot arrive checked against one
// geometry only.
function requireArenaCanServe(what String, chunks ParsedInt)
	requireArenaCanServeOnLane(what, chunks: chunks, hasPageReservation: true)
	requireArenaCanServeOnLane(what, chunks: chunks, hasPageReservation: false)
end 'requireArenaCanServe'

function requireArenaCanServeOnLane(what String, chunks ParsedInt, hasPageReservation bool)
	let available = slabArenaAllocatableChunks(hasPageReservation)
	if chunks > available 'unsatisfiable'
		panic("slab runtime: {what} needs {chunks} chunk(s) and an arena on {arenaLaneLabel(hasPageReservation)} has {available} after its metadata, so `__slab_arena_alloc_chunks` would abort at run time")
	end 'unsatisfiable'
end 'requireArenaCanServeOnLane'

// Which lane a bound above is talking about. The two produce the same sentence with different numbers, and
// "18 against 63" is only readable once the reader is told which arena was expected — `SlabArena`'s own
// `arenaLaneName` makes the identical argument about its identical pair of panics.
function arenaLaneLabel(hasPageReservation bool) returns String
	if hasPageReservation 'reserves'
		return "a reserving lane"
	end 'reserves'

	return "the non-reserving lane"
end 'arenaLaneLabel'

// One mcache row: one span pointer per class. DERIVED from the class count, which is v1's
// `SLAB_MCACHE_ROW_STRIDE = 144` written as what it is instead of as a number a regenerated ladder
// invalidates in silence.
function slabMcacheRowStride() returns ParsedInt
	return slabClassCount() * WordBytes
end 'slabMcacheRowStride'

function slabStateMcacheOffset() returns ParsedInt
	return SlabStateMcentralOffset + slabClassCount() * WordBytes
end 'slabStateMcacheOffset'

function slabStateBytes() returns ParsedInt
	return slabStateMcacheOffset() + SlabMaxShards * slabMcacheRowStride()
end 'slabStateBytes'

function slabStateChunks() returns ParsedInt
	return (slabStateBytes() + SlabPageBytes - 1) / SlabPageBytes
end 'slabStateChunks'

// --- Shared emitters ---

// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE PLACE THE SMALL/LARGE BOUNDARY IS SPELLED.** v1 says of its `__slab_rounded_size` that it
// *"mirrors the two routing arms of `__slab_alloc` exactly"* — which is one fact written twice, and the
// failure mode is a sizing function that reports a class for a request the allocator sends to the OS. Both
// callers branch through here, so they cannot disagree about where the boundary is.
//
// ⚠ **THE PAGE ROUNDING ON THE OS-DIRECT ARM IS THE OTHER HALF OF THE SAME AGREEMENT**, and it is not a
// second function: both arms call `MmRuntime.emitAlignUp` naming `SlabPageBytes`, so the width they round
// to is one named constant rather than two spellings of 8192.
function emitSlabSizeRouting(module StdModule, block IrBlock, size ValueId, osDirect IrBlock, small IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	let maxSmall = emitConst(module, block: block, value: SlabMaxSmallSize, ids: ids)
	let isLarge = emitCmp(module, block: block, a: size, b: maxSmall, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(block, op: StdOp.condBranch(isLarge, thenBlockId: osDirect.id, elseBlockId: small.id))
end 'emitSlabSizeRouting'

// The packed geometry word for `classIdx` — ONE indexed `.rdata` load, which is the whole of what storing
// `class_idx` alone costs. See `SlabClassGeomSlotShift`.
function emitClassGeometry(module StdModule, block IrBlock, classIdx ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let base = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.rdataAddr(base, label: SlabClassGeomLabel.toByteArray()))
	return emitWordSlotLoad(module, block: block, base: base, index: classIdx, ids: ids)
end 'emitClassGeometry'

function emitGeomSlotSize(module StdModule, block IrBlock, geom ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let mask = emitConst(module, block: block, value: SlabClassGeomSlotMask, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: geom, b: mask, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
end 'emitGeomSlotSize'

function emitGeomTotalSlots(module StdModule, block IrBlock, geom ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let shift = emitConst(module, block: block, value: SlabClassGeomSlotShift, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: geom, b: shift, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shr, ids: ids)
end 'emitGeomTotalSlots'

// The address of `state`'s word-indexed slot at `arrayOffset + index * 8` — the mcentral head for a class,
// and (with the row folded into `index`) an mcache entry. Both arrays are word-indexed off one base, so the
// arithmetic is written once and the callers differ only in what they index by.
function emitStateSlotAddr(module StdModule, block IrBlock, state ValueId, arrayOffset ParsedInt, index ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let arrayBase = emitConst(module, block: block, value: arrayOffset, ids: ids)
	let fromBase = emitBin(module, block: block, a: state, b: arrayBase, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	return emitWordSlotAddr(module, block: block, base: fromBase, index: index, ids: ids)
end 'emitStateSlotAddr'

// `ceil(slotSize * totalSlots / SlabPageBytes)` — the chunks a span's slot region occupies.
//
// ⭐ **ONE SPELLING, BECAUSE THE CUT AND THE DESTRUCTION MUST AGREE TO THE CHUNK.** `__slab_refill` computes
// it to ask the arena for a run and to register that many pages in the reverse map; `__slab_span_destroy`
// computes it to hand the same run back and to unregister the same pages. Spelled twice, a span could be
// released one chunk short — leaving a chunk claimed forever — or one chunk long, releasing a chunk that
// belongs to its neighbour.
function emitSpanChunkCount(module StdModule, block IrBlock, slotSize ValueId, totalSlots ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let dataBytes = emitBin(module, block: block, a: slotSize, b: totalSlots, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	let pageBump = emitConst(module, block: block, value: SlabPageBytes - 1, ids: ids)
	let raised = emitBin(module, block: block, a: dataBytes, b: pageBump, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let pageShift = emitConst(module, block: block, value: SlabPageShift, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: raised, b: pageShift, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shr, ids: ids)
end 'emitSpanChunkCount'

// The mcache entry `mcache[row][classIdx]`. `row * stride` is a MULTIPLY rather than a shift because the
// stride is `classCount * 8` and the class count is Go's 68, which is not a power of two — v1's 144 was not
// either, and it spells the same multiply.
function emitMcacheSlotAddr(module StdModule, block IrBlock, state ValueId, row ValueId, classIdx ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let stride = emitConst(module, block: block, value: slabMcacheRowStride(), ids: ids)
	let rowOffset = emitBin(module, block: block, a: row, b: stride, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	let rowBase = emitBin(module, block: block, a: state, b: rowOffset, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	return emitStateSlotAddr(module, block: block, state: rowBase, arrayOffset: slabStateMcacheOffset(), index: classIdx, ids: ids)
end 'emitMcacheSlotAddr'

// --- The sharding emitters ---
//
// ⭐ **THESE FIVE ARE WHAT KEEP "WHICH P AM I", "WHICH ROW IS THAT", "TAKE THE LOCK", "PUSH ONTO A SPAN" AND
// "PUSH ONTO A QUEUE" SPELLED ONCE EACH.** v1 writes its ownership gate out TWICE inside one function and
// says so in the second copy's comment (*"Same ownership gate as the initial check"*); that is the shape a
// shared emitter exists to make impossible.

// ⭐ **THE ONE DOOR TO "WHICH P IS RUNNING ME" — a P pointer, or `MspanOwningPNone` for an OS thread that
// owns none.** It answers in `pId`, the merge block's single argument. ⚠ In an UNSHARDED build it is a
// CONSTANT and no block at all: there is no `__sched_procs`, no `__sched_tls_teb_offset` `.data` word to
// read (`schedRuntimeGlobals` gates it on the same bit) and no second thread, so the honest answer is known
// at build time and the hot path pays nothing to be told it.
//
// ⭐⭐ **TWO LOADS AND NO CALL INTO THE SCHEDULER: `gs:[__sched_tls_teb_offset]` IS `TlsGetValue`.**
// `SchedRuntime.SchedTlsTebOffsetLabel` carries the derivation of that precomputed offset in full.
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT WAS `__slab_shard_p`, A FUNCTION, UNTIL EC8.** Six ops behind a `call`/`ret` pair and a frame,
// on the path of every allocation AND every free of a SHARDED build — and a sharded build is what the
// compiler itself is (its spec runner drives a worker pool over `async`). The ops are unchanged; what went
// is the frame. Its two callers were both in this file, so no name outside it could depend on the symbol.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE ZERO TEST IS NOT DEFENSIVE — IT IS THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN A P AND A WILD LOAD.** Before
// `__sched_init_procs` runs, the offset word reads 0, and `gs:[0]` is `NT_TIB.ExceptionList` — a NON-NULL
// pointer on Windows — so reading through it hands back a garbage "P" whose `POffId` load faults. The
// allocator reaches this path on the very first allocation of every program, which is long before any
// `async`, so that is the COMMON case rather than an edge. `SchedRuntime.emitLoadCurrentGtOrZero` and
// `X64GtRuntime.emitLoadCurrentPOrBail` make the identical two-zero argument for the identical read.
//
// ⚠ **BOTH ZEROS ANSWER THE SAME THING HERE, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.** *"No scheduler yet"* and *"an OS
// thread that owns no P"* (the IOCP completion thread, for its whole life) are different facts, but the
// allocator's question is only *"is there a P whose row I may own"* — and for both the answer is no. The one
// place the difference matters is whether another thread can EXIST, which is a separate predicate
// (`emitSlabSchedulerExists`) asked only where the lock decision needs it.
function emitSlabCurrentP(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, sharded bool, pId ValueId, ids ValueMinter, label ByteArray) returns IrBlock
	if not sharded 'noScheduler'
		module.appendOp(cur, op: StdOp.const(pId, value: MspanOwningPNone, valueType: StdType.i64))
		return cur
	end 'noScheduler'

	var haveOffset = module.addBlock(func, label: label)
	var merge = module.addBlock(func, label: label)
	merge.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(pId, argType: StdType.i64))

	let tebOffset = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: cur, label: SchedTlsTebOffsetLabel, ids: ids)
	let never = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let noScheduler = emitCmp(module, block: cur, a: tebOffset, b: never, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	let noProc = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: MspanOwningPNone, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(noScheduler, thenBlockId: merge.id, elseBlockId: haveOffset.id))
	cur.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(merge.id, argIds: argsOf(noProc)))

	let p = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(haveOffset, op: StdOp.tlsSlotLoad(p, tebOffsetId: tebOffset))
	module.setTerminator(haveOffset, op: StdOp.branch(merge.id))
	haveOffset.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(merge.id, argIds: argsOf(p)))

	return merge
end 'emitSlabCurrentP'

// ⭐⭐ **HAS A SCHEDULER EVER EXISTED IN THIS PROCESS?** — the one question that separates *"I am the only
// thread"* from *"another OS thread may be inside the allocator right now"*, and the gate on both of the
// lock's fast-path arms. `__sched_tls_teb_offset` is zero until `__sched_init_procs` computes it and never
// returns to zero, so a non-zero read is a permanent, monotonic *"threads are possible"*.
//
// ⭐ **IT IS THE RIGHT PREDICATE AND NOT MERELY A CONVENIENT ONE.** v1 asks `__slab_proc_at(0) != 0` — is
// there a P[0]? — which is the same instant in the program's life reached through an array shv2 does not
// need to export. The word is exported already, because it is how every P read starts.
function emitSlabSchedulerExists(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let tebOffset = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: block, label: SchedTlsTebOffsetLabel, ids: ids)
	let never = emitConst(module, block: block, value: 0, ids: ids)
	return emitCmp(module, block: block, a: tebOffset, b: never, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
end 'emitSlabSchedulerExists'

// ⭐⭐ **THE mcache ROW FOR A P — `clamp(P->id)`, with NO P mapping to `SlabRawShardRow` AND NEVER TO 0.**
// `SlabRawShardRow`'s own note carries v1's measured reason for the second half. The `>= SlabMaxShards - 1`
// arm is the first: a host with 256+ processors would otherwise index past the mcache entirely.
//
// ⚠ **IT CLAMPS TO `SlabRawShardRow` RATHER THAN TO `SlabMaxShards - 1` — WHICH IS THE SAME NUMBER AND A
// DIFFERENT DECISION, AND IT CLOSES A HOLE v1 LEAVES OPEN.** v1 caps such a P at its last row and admits in
// the comment that it *"would only collide there"* with the raw threads — a real, if remote, corruption,
// because those Ps would then share a row with no lock and no single writer. Clamping to the RAW row instead
// makes them raw-row citizens, and the raw row's rule (`buildSlabAllocEntry`: serialise under the lock) then
// covers them for free. There is no third case to remember.
//
// It terminates `cur` and answers in `rowId`, the merge block's single argument — the branching-emitter
// shape this runtime uses everywhere, the Std tier having no select.
function emitSlabShardRow(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, myP ValueId, sharded bool, rowId ValueId, ids ValueMinter, label ByteArray) returns IrBlock
	if not sharded 'noScheduler'
		module.appendOp(cur, op: StdOp.const(rowId, value: SlabRawShardRow, valueType: StdType.i64))
		return cur
	end 'noScheduler'

	var havePid = module.addBlock(func, label: label)
	var capped = module.addBlock(func, label: label)
	var merge = module.addBlock(func, label: label)
	merge.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(rowId, argType: StdType.i64))

	// ⚠⚠ **`<= MspanOwningPNone` RATHER THAN `==`, WHICH MAKES THIS PREDICATE TOTAL AND NOT MERELY
	// CORRECT-UNDER-A-PRECONDITION.** The alloc path only ever passes a P pointer or `None`; the other
	// caller — `__slab_span_return` — passes an mspan's `owning_p`, whose third value is
	// `MspanOwningPParked` (-1). Under `==` that value would fall through to the `POffId` load and read
	// address 0x1F. It is unreachable by construction (every caller of `__slab_free_to_span` has
	// established the span is not parked), but a predicate that answers for all three values costs the
	// same one compare and cannot be broken by a future caller — and "parked" answering the RAW row is
	// harmless there, because the row is then only used for a `== span` test that cannot match.
	let rawRow = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: SlabRawShardRow, ids: ids)
	let noneOwner = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: MspanOwningPNone, ids: ids)
	let noProc = emitCmp(module, block: cur, a: myP, b: noneOwner, pred: StdCmpPred.le, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(noProc, thenBlockId: merge.id, elseBlockId: havePid.id))
	cur.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(merge.id, argIds: argsOf(rawRow)))

	// A `BranchEdge` is keyed by its TARGET, so the capped arm needs a block of its own rather than a second
	// edge to `merge` — two edges naming one target cannot be told apart by the argument lookup.
	let id = emitLoadField(module, block: havePid, record: myP, offset: POffId, ids: ids)
	let firstSharedRow = emitConst(module, block: havePid, value: SlabRawShardRow, ids: ids)
	let beyond = emitCmp(module, block: havePid, a: id, b: firstSharedRow, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(havePid, op: StdOp.condBranch(beyond, thenBlockId: capped.id, elseBlockId: merge.id))
	havePid.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(merge.id, argIds: argsOf(id)))

	let sharedRow = emitConst(module, block: capped, value: SlabRawShardRow, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(capped, op: StdOp.branch(merge.id))
	capped.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(merge.id, argIds: argsOf(sharedRow)))

	return merge
end 'emitSlabShardRow'

// The global slow-path lock, at its fixed offset in the state region. One spelling, because a lock reached
// through two different address computations is two locks the day one of them is wrong.
function emitSlabLockAddr(module StdModule, block IrBlock, state ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let offset = emitConst(module, block: block, value: SlabStateLockOffset as ParsedInt, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: state, b: offset, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
end 'emitSlabLockAddr'

// ⚠ **A `sharded: false` ENTER/LEAVE EMITS NOTHING, AND THAT IS WHY THE CALLERS CARRY NO `if`.** The one
// program shape that reaches this file without a scheduler is also the one that cannot lower `osLockEnter`
// (wasm, arm64), so the gate has to exist; putting it inside the two emitters means the slow-path bodies
// read as though the lock were unconditional, which — for every program that has a second thread — it is.
function emitSlabLockEnter(module StdModule, block IrBlock, state ValueId, sharded bool, ids ValueMinter)
	if not sharded 'singleThreaded'
		return
	end 'singleThreaded'

	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.osLockEnter(emitSlabLockAddr(module, block: block, state: state, ids: ids)))
end 'emitSlabLockEnter'

function emitSlabLockLeave(module StdModule, block IrBlock, state ValueId, sharded bool, ids ValueMinter)
	if not sharded 'singleThreaded'
		return
	end 'singleThreaded'

	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.osLockLeave(emitSlabLockAddr(module, block: block, state: state, ids: ids)))
end 'emitSlabLockLeave'

// ⭐⭐ **CAS-PUSH `slot` ONTO P `target`'s REMOTE-FREE QUEUE — a Treiber stack at `POffRemoteFreeHead`, many
// producers and exactly one consumer.** The link rides in the slot's own first word, which is the same word
// the span free list threads its links through and the same word the allocation pop overwrites when it
// hands the slot back out, so the queue costs no storage of its own.
//
// ⛔⛔ **NO FENCE IS NEEDED HERE AND NONE MAY BE ADDED "TO BE SAFE".** The store of the old head into
// `[slot]` must be visible to the DRAINER before the CAS publishes `slot`, and on both lanes shv2 emits the
// CAS on, the CAS *is* that barrier: x64's `lock cmpxchg` is a full barrier by architecture, and arm64's
// `StLXR` (`TargetOp.arm64AtomicCas`'s LL/SC pair) is a store-RELEASE, which orders every prior store before
// it. A speculative `mfence`/`dmb` would be a cost with no property to buy.
//
// ⚠ `StdOp.atomicCas`'s result is the SUCCESS BOOLEAN, not the prior value — the op's own header states the
// convention, and a loop written for the other one spins forever on its first success.
function emitSlabRemotePush(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, target ValueId, slot ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	var retry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rpush")
	let done = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rpushed")

	let offset = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: POffRemoteFreeHead as ParsedInt, ids: ids)
	let headAddr = emitBin(module, block: cur, a: target, b: offset, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.branch(retry.id))

	let head = emitLoadField(module, block: retry, record: headAddr, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: retry, record: slot, offset: 0, value: head)
	let won = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(retry, op: StdOp.atomicCas(won, addrId: headAddr, expectedId: head, replacementId: slot))
	let lost = emitConst(module, block: retry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let contended = emitCmp(module, block: retry, a: won, b: lost, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(retry, op: StdOp.condBranch(contended, thenBlockId: retry.id, elseBlockId: done.id))

	return done
end 'emitSlabRemotePush'

// --- The bodies ---

// ⭐⭐ **"HAS THE STATE REGION BEEN COMMITTED YET?" — the `.data` word read and tested, ONE SPELLING.**
// It answers the PREDICATE and leaves the word itself in `currentId` and its address in `addrId`, both of
// which the callers need for different things: `buildSlabStateBase` returns the word on its hit arm and
// stores THROUGH the address on its commit arm; `emitSlabStateBase` carries the word into a merge block.
//
// ⚠ **THE TWO CALLERS CANNOT COLLAPSE INTO ONE, WHICH IS PRECISELY WHY THIS EXISTS.** `emitSlabStateBase`'s
// COLD arm is a call to `__slab_state_base`, so the function cannot be written as one emission of the
// emitter — it would call itself. The read is therefore the one part that would otherwise be written twice,
// and two spellings of it is how one of them ends up testing a sentinel the other stopped writing.
function emitSlabStateWordRead(module StdModule, block IrBlock, addrId ValueId, currentId ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.globalAddr(addrId, label: SlabStateLabel))
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(currentId, addrId: addrId, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))

	let absent = emitConst(module, block: block, value: SlabStateUncommitted, ids: ids)
	return emitCmp(module, block: block, a: currentId, b: absent, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
end 'emitSlabStateWordRead'

// `__slab_state_base() -> base` — the allocator's mutable state, committed on first use.
//
// ⭐ **THE REGION IS CHUNKS FROM THE ARENA, NOT A FRESH `osAllocPages`.** This sits ON the arena, so taking
// its chunks out of a mapping the process has already committed costs no additional commit at all. (The
// free-list table `MmRuntime` carried until S4 went straight to the OS instead, because it sat beneath
// nothing and had no arena to ask.)
//
// ⚠ **THE ZEROING IS NOT OPTIONAL AND THE ARENA'S OWN CONTRACT IS WHY.** A chunk is fresh OS memory only
// the first time it is handed out — the arena's scan is first-fit over a bitmap, so a freed low chunk is
// reused before a virgin high one. `SlabArena.emitLazyMapLevel` zeroes its level tables for exactly this
// reason and states it; an unzeroed mcache reads a dead chunk's bytes as a span pointer.
//
// ⭐ **AND IT IS WHERE THE SLOW-PATH LOCK IS BORN.** See `SlabStateLockOffset` for why the CRITICAL_SECTION
// lives in this region rather than in a `.data` word, and for the argument that this arm runs while the main
// thread is still the process's only one. The `memFill` above it is what makes `osLockInit` legal: it hands
// the kernel a zeroed region rather than a dead chunk's bytes.
function buildSlabStateBase(module StdModule, sharded bool)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SlabStateName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var commit = module.addBlock(func, label: b"commit")
	var have = module.addBlock(func, label: b"have")

	let stateAddr = ids.mint()
	let current = ids.mint()
	let uncommitted = emitSlabStateWordRead(module, block: entry, addrId: stateAddr, currentId: current, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(uncommitted, thenBlockId: commit.id, elseBlockId: have.id))

	let chunks = emitConst(module, block: commit, value: slabStateChunks(), ids: ids)
	let base = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(commit, op: StdOp.call(base, callee: SlabArenaAllocChunksName, args: argsOf(chunks)))
	let stateBytes = emitConst(module, block: commit, value: slabStateBytes(), ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(commit, op: StdOp.memFill(base, byteCountId: stateBytes, value: 0))
	if sharded 'theSlowPathLock'
		module.appendOp(commit, op: StdOp.osLockInit(emitSlabLockAddr(module, block: commit, state: base, ids: ids)))
	end 'theSlowPathLock'
	// LAST: the `.data` word is what every other thread tests to decide the region exists, so publishing it
	// before the lock inside it is initialised would hand a second thread an uninitialised CRITICAL_SECTION.
	emitStoreField(module, block: commit, record: stateAddr, offset: 0, value: base)
	module.setTerminator(commit, op: StdOp.ret(base))

	module.setTerminator(have, op: StdOp.ret(current))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSlabStateBase'

// ⭐⭐ **THE SAME ANSWER WITHOUT A FRAME: the `.data` word, tested, with the COMMIT left as the call it
// already is** (EC8). It answers in `stateId`, the merge block's single argument.
//
// ⭐ **THE HOT ARM IS FOUR OPS AND THE COLD ONE IS A CALL, WHICH IS THE WHOLE OF WHY THIS IS NOT A SECOND
// COPY OF THE BODY.** The region is committed by the FIRST allocation a process makes and never released,
// so every subsequent allocation and every free reads a non-zero word and is done. Reproducing the commit
// here — the arena request, the `memFill`, the `osLockInit`, the publishing store, in the order that
// publishes LAST — would be that sequence written twice, and it is the sequence whose ORDER is the
// argument. So the cold arm calls `__slab_state_base`, which re-reads the word and commits it; that second
// read costs one load on the once-per-process path, and it is the SAME read — `emitSlabStateWordRead` is
// the one spelling both this and the function's own entry use.
function emitSlabStateBase(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, stateId ValueId, ids ValueMinter, label ByteArray) returns IrBlock
	var commit = module.addBlock(func, label: label)
	var merge = module.addBlock(func, label: label)
	merge.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(stateId, argType: StdType.i64))

	let stateAddr = ids.mint()
	let current = ids.mint()
	let uncommitted = emitSlabStateWordRead(module, block: cur, addrId: stateAddr, currentId: current, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(uncommitted, thenBlockId: commit.id, elseBlockId: merge.id))
	cur.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(merge.id, argIds: argsOf(current)))

	let committed = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(commit, op: StdOp.call(committed, callee: SlabStateName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	module.setTerminator(commit, op: StdOp.branch(merge.id))
	commit.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(merge.id, argIds: argsOf(committed)))

	return merge
end 'emitSlabStateBase'

// **`classIdx` = the smallest class whose slot holds `size` bytes, in O(1)** — emitted INTO the caller's
// blocks, answering in `classId`, the merge block's single argument.
//
// ⛔⛔ **v1 SCANS THE LADDER AND THAT DOES NOT PORT.** Its `__slab_class_index_for` walks up to 18 classes
// per allocation calling `__slab_class_size` — itself an 18-arm if-ladder — so a 1 KiB request costs ~15
// compares before a byte is served, on the hot path of every allocation in the language. Go answers the
// same question with TWO reverse tables and one indexed byte load, which is what this emits.
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT WAS A FUNCTION (`__slab_class_index_for`) UNTIL EC8, AND A NINE-OP LEAF IS NOT WORTH A FRAME.**
// The whole body is two compares, four ALU ops, an `.rdata` address and a byte load — LESS than the
// prologue, the argument move, the `call`, the `ret` and the epilogue that reached it, and it sits on the
// path of every allocation in the language. Emitting it inline is what makes `__slab_alloc`'s hit path one
// frame; the two callers (the allocator's entry and the sizing answer) are both inside this file, so there
// is no name for anything else to have depended on.
//
// ⚠ **THE TWO TABLES ARE DERIVED, NOT TRANSCRIBED** (`SlabClassTable.slabSizeToClass8Table`), and their
// defining property is asserted at build time.
//
// ⚠ **A CALLER MUST HAVE ROUTED `size > SlabMaxSmallSize` AWAY** — `emitSlabSizeRouting` is what does it.
// A request of 0 reaches class 0, the ladder's sentinel, whose span holds no objects; `__slab_refill`'s
// guard is where that is caught, on the COLD path, so the hot path pays nothing for a case no caller has.
function emitSlabClassIndex(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, size ValueId, classId ValueId, ids ValueMinter, label ByteArray) returns IrBlock
	var smallTable = module.addBlock(func, label: label)
	var largeTable = module.addBlock(func, label: label)
	var merge = module.addBlock(func, label: label)
	merge.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(classId, argType: StdType.i64))

	let tableBoundary = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: SlabSmallTableMaxSize, ids: ids)
	let aboveSmallTable = emitCmp(module, block: cur, a: size, b: tableBoundary, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(aboveSmallTable, thenBlockId: largeTable.id, elseBlockId: smallTable.id))

	let small = emitReverseTableLookup(module, block: smallTable, size: size, subtract: 0, divShift: SlabSmallSizeDivShift, divisor: SlabSmallSizeDiv, label: SlabSizeToClass8Label, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(smallTable, op: StdOp.branch(merge.id))
	smallTable.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(merge.id, argIds: argsOf(small)))

	let large = emitReverseTableLookup(module, block: largeTable, size: size, subtract: SlabSmallSizeMax, divShift: SlabLargeSizeDivShift, divisor: SlabLargeSizeDiv, label: SlabSizeToClass128Label, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(largeTable, op: StdOp.branch(merge.id))
	largeTable.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(merge.id, argIds: argsOf(large)))

	return merge
end 'emitSlabClassIndex'

// One reverse-table read: `table[ceil((size - subtract) / divisor)]`. Both arms are the same four ops over
// different constants, which is why they are one emitter — writing them out twice is how one of the two
// ends up dividing by the other's stride.
//
// The rounding is `(x + divisor - 1) shr divShift`, and it is exact for both arms because every value
// reaching either is positive: the small arm sees `size <= 1016` and the large arm sees `size >= 1017`, so
// `size - 1024 + 127 >= 120`.
function emitReverseTableLookup(module StdModule, block IrBlock, size ValueId, subtract ParsedInt, divShift ParsedInt, divisor ParsedInt, label String, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let bias = emitConst(module, block: block, value: divisor - 1 - subtract, ids: ids)
	let raised = emitBin(module, block: block, a: size, b: bias, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let shift = emitConst(module, block: block, value: divShift, ids: ids)
	let index = emitBin(module, block: block, a: raised, b: shift, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shr, ids: ids)

	let base = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.rdataAddr(base, label: label.toByteArray()))
	return emitLoadByteIndexed(module, block: block, base: base, index: index, ids: ids)
end 'emitReverseTableLookup'

// `__slab_rounded_size(size) -> bytes` — the number of bytes `__slab_alloc(size)` actually serves.
//
// ⭐ **IT SHARES THE ROUTING WITH THE ALLOCATOR (`emitSlabSizeRouting`) AND THE PAGE ALIGNMENT WITH THE
// OS-DIRECT PATH (`emitAlignUp` over `SlabPageBytes`)**, which is the whole of what v1's *"mirrors the two
// routing arms of `__slab_alloc` exactly"* was asking for and not getting.
//
// ⚠ **IT HAS NO CALLER IN shv2 TODAY, AND SINCE S4 THERE IS ONE WAITING FOR IT.** v1's caller is the
// inline-small-array capacity reclaim, which turns a class's slack into spare elements. shv2's array records
// have no such path yet — but now that every managed box is a slab slot, the slack between an array's
// requested bytes and the class that serves them is real and measurable, so the reclaim is a question this
// compiler can finally ask. `DFE` drops the function from every program until something does, so it costs
// nothing emitted, and the reason it is here rather than absent is the shared emitters above — they are what
// makes the sizing answer and the allocator's routing one fact rather than two that must be kept in step.
function buildSlabRoundedSize(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SlabRoundedSizeName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var osDirect = module.addBlock(func, label: b"osdirect")
	var small = module.addBlock(func, label: b"small")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let size = 0 as ValueId

	emitSlabSizeRouting(module, block: entry, size: size, osDirect: osDirect, small: small, ids: ids)

	// The OS-direct answer is the mapping's payload: the request plus the size prefix, rounded to a page,
	// less the prefix again — the bytes a caller may actually use.
	let prefix = emitConst(module, block: osDirect, value: OsDirectPrefixBytes, ids: ids)
	let withPrefix = emitBin(module, block: osDirect, a: size, b: prefix, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let mapped = emitAlignUp(module, block: osDirect, value: withPrefix, width: SlabPageBytes, ids: ids)
	let usable = emitBin(module, block: osDirect, a: mapped, b: prefix, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(osDirect, op: StdOp.ret(usable))

	let class = ids.mint()
	var classReady = emitSlabClassIndex(module, func: func, cur: small, size: size, classId: class, ids: ids, label: b"cls")
	let geom = emitClassGeometry(module, block: classReady, classIdx: class, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(classReady, op: StdOp.ret(emitGeomSlotSize(module, block: classReady, geom: geom, ids: ids)))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSlabRoundedSize'

// `__slab_meta_alloc() -> span` — one mspan header, off the free list if there is one and off the metadata
// slab's bump cursor otherwise.
//
// ⭐ **THE FREE LIST IS S6's, AND IT HAS A PRODUCER NOW.** This note used to say a free list here would be
// "a structure with no producer", which was exactly right while nothing destroyed a span — a fully-free span
// parked in mcentral and was reused. The scavenger destroys one, and without a list to put its header on,
// every span the scavenger reclaims would leak 64 bytes of metadata slab for the life of the process. It is
// v1's `__slab_meta_free`, arriving for v1's reason at the rung that creates it.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE SLOT IS NOT ASSUMED ZERO, AND THE FREE LIST IS WHY THAT RULE FINALLY BITES.** `__slab_refill`
// writes every mspan field regardless — v1's rule stated in full: *"an uninitialised field here is STALE
// DATA, not zero"* — which is what keeps this function free of any zeroing at all. A recycled header carries
// the previous span's fields AND the list link this file wrote over its first word, so a refill that left
// ONE field unwritten would produce a span describing a chunk run that belongs to somebody else.
//
// ⚠ **PRECONDITION: THE CALLER HOLDS `__slab_lock`.** The metadata slab's bump cursor and free list are
// shared by every P; both callers (`__slab_refill`, `__slab_span_destroy`) are already inside it, so taking
// it here would be a recursive re-entry paying for a fact the caller has established. It is stated rather
// than taken for `__slab_span_destroy`'s reason: a precondition a caller can read is cheaper than a lock
// every caller re-acquires.
function buildSlabMetaAlloc(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SlabMetaAllocName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var recycled = module.addBlock(func, label: b"recycled")
	var bumpPath = module.addBlock(func, label: b"bump")
	var take = module.addBlock(func, label: b"take")
	var fresh = module.addBlock(func, label: b"fresh")

	let state = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(state, callee: SlabStateName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))

	// POP FIRST. A recycled header is warm and costs one load; the bump path is what runs while the metadata
	// slab is still growing, which is every program that never scavenges.
	let listHead = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: state, offset: SlabStateMetaFreeOffset, ids: ids)
	let noneFree = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let listEmpty = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: listHead, b: noneFree, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(listEmpty, thenBlockId: bumpPath.id, elseBlockId: recycled.id))

	// The link lives in the dead header's FIRST word — `base_addr`, which `__slab_refill` overwrites — so
	// unlinking is one load and one store and the slot needs no clearing.
	let following = emitLoadField(module, block: recycled, record: listHead, offset: MspanBaseAddrOffset, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: recycled, record: state, offset: SlabStateMetaFreeOffset, value: following)
	module.setTerminator(recycled, op: StdOp.ret(listHead))

	let bump = emitLoadField(module, block: bumpPath, record: state, offset: SlabStateMetaBumpOffset, ids: ids)
	let slotBytes = emitConst(module, block: bumpPath, value: MspanSlotBytes, ids: ids)
	let advanced = emitBin(module, block: bumpPath, a: bump, b: slotBytes, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let limit = emitLoadField(module, block: bumpPath, record: state, offset: SlabStateMetaEndOffset, ids: ids)
	// The initial state is `bump == 0` and `limit == 0`, so the first call fails this test and takes the
	// fresh-chunk arm — the `.data` zero IS the initialisation, exactly as it is for the arena list head.
	let fits = emitCmp(module, block: bumpPath, a: advanced, b: limit, pred: StdCmpPred.le, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(bumpPath, op: StdOp.condBranch(fits, thenBlockId: take.id, elseBlockId: fresh.id))

	emitStoreField(module, block: take, record: state, offset: SlabStateMetaBumpOffset, value: advanced)
	module.setTerminator(take, op: StdOp.ret(bump))

	let one = emitConst(module, block: fresh, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let chunk = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(fresh, op: StdOp.call(chunk, callee: SlabArenaAllocChunksName, args: argsOf(one)))
	let firstFree = emitBin(module, block: fresh, a: chunk, b: slotBytes, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: fresh, record: state, offset: SlabStateMetaBumpOffset, value: firstFree)
	let chunkBytes = emitConst(module, block: fresh, value: SlabPageBytes, ids: ids)
	let chunkEnd = emitBin(module, block: fresh, a: chunk, b: chunkBytes, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: fresh, record: state, offset: SlabStateMetaEndOffset, value: chunkEnd)
	module.setTerminator(fresh, op: StdOp.ret(chunk))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSlabMetaAlloc'

// `__slab_meta_free(span)` — put a destroyed span's header back on the metadata free list.
//
// The link goes in the header's FIRST word (`base_addr`), which is the field `__slab_refill` overwrites
// first on the way back out — so the list costs no storage of its own and a recycled header needs no
// clearing. The same trick the SPAN free list plays with a dead slot's first word, one layer up.
//
// ⚠ **THE HEADER'S CHUNK IS NEVER RELEASED, ONLY ITS SLOTS ARE REUSED.** The metadata slab bump-allocates
// out of whole chunks and this list hands the SLOTS back; the chunks themselves stay claimed for the life of
// the process. That is deliberate: a metadata chunk holds up to 128 headers belonging to unrelated spans, so
// it can only be released when every one of them is dead, which is a second free-list-of-chunks problem for
// a structure that is 0.8% of the heap it describes.
//
// ⚠ **PRECONDITION: THE CALLER HOLDS `__slab_lock`** — see `__slab_meta_alloc`, whose cursor this list
// shares.
function buildSlabMetaFree(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SlabMetaFreeName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let span = 0 as ValueId

	let state = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(state, callee: SlabStateName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	let head = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: state, offset: SlabStateMetaFreeOffset, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: entry, record: span, offset: MspanBaseAddrOffset, value: head)
	emitStoreField(module, block: entry, record: state, offset: SlabStateMetaFreeOffset, value: span)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSlabMetaFree'

// `__slab_refill(classIdx, myP, row) -> span` — put a span with free slots in `row`'s mcache entry, stamp it
// as `myP`'s, and answer it.
//
// Two tiers, v1's: take the head of `mcentral[classIdx]` if there is one, else cut a fresh span out of the
// arena. Both ends install into the mcache and stamp the owner.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE WHOLE BODY RUNS UNDER `__slab_lock`, AND EVERYTHING IT CALLS INHERITS THAT.** It reads and
// rewrites an mcentral head, drives the metadata slab, cuts chunks out of the arena and registers pages in
// the reverse map — four structures every P shares. The fast path above it takes nothing.
//
// ⚠ **`myP` AND `row` ARE PARAMETERS RATHER THAN RE-DERIVED, WHICH IS A DIVERGENCE FROM v1 WITH A REASON.**
// v1's `__slab_refill_mcache(classIdx, rawId)` re-clamps the id to a row, so the caller's row and the
// refill's row are two computations of one fact — and if they ever disagreed the span would be stamped for
// one P and installed in another's row, which is precisely the corruption the ownership gate exists to catch.
// One derivation, in `__slab_alloc`, passed down.
function buildSlabRefill(module StdModule, sharded bool)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SlabRefillName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 3, paramTypes: threeI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(3)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var takePartial = module.addBlock(func, label: b"partial")
	var grow = module.addBlock(func, label: b"grow")
	var emptyClass = module.addBlock(func, label: b"emptyclass")
	var cut = module.addBlock(func, label: b"cut")
	var install = module.addBlock(func, label: b"install")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 3)
	let classIdx = 0 as ValueId
	let myP = 1 as ValueId
	let row = 2 as ValueId

	let state = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(state, callee: SlabStateName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	emitSlabLockEnter(module, block: entry, state: state, sharded: sharded, ids: ids)
	let mcentralSlot = emitStateSlotAddr(module, block: entry, state: state, arrayOffset: SlabStateMcentralOffset as ParsedInt, index: classIdx, ids: ids)
	let mcacheSlot = emitMcacheSlotAddr(module, block: entry, state: state, row: row, classIdx: classIdx, ids: ids)
	let partial = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: mcentralSlot, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	let none = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let noPartial = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: partial, b: none, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(noPartial, thenBlockId: grow.id, elseBlockId: takePartial.id))

	// Unlink the head. Its free list and its unconsumed bump region both survive the park (see the header's
	// "the case that looks wrong"), so there is nothing here to rebuild.
	let following = emitLoadField(module, block: takePartial, record: partial, offset: MspanNextSpanOffset, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: takePartial, record: mcentralSlot, offset: 0, value: following)
	emitStoreField(module, block: takePartial, record: partial, offset: MspanNextSpanOffset, value: none)
	module.setTerminator(takePartial, op: StdOp.branch(install.id))
	takePartial.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(install.id, argIds: argsOf(partial)))

	let geom = emitClassGeometry(module, block: grow, classIdx: classIdx, ids: ids)
	let slotSize = emitGeomSlotSize(module, block: grow, geom: geom, ids: ids)
	let totalSlots = emitGeomTotalSlots(module, block: grow, geom: geom, ids: ids)
	// Class 0 is the ladder's sentinel: size 0, no objects. Only a zero-byte request reaches it, and it can
	// only reach it through the class lookup, so this is where such a request is refused — on the COLD path,
	// which is why the class lookup needs no guard of its own.
	let oneSlot = emitConst(module, block: grow, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let servesNothing = emitCmp(module, block: grow, a: totalSlots, b: oneSlot, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(grow, op: StdOp.condBranch(servesNothing, thenBlockId: emptyClass.id, elseBlockId: cut.id))

	emitRuntimeAbort(module, func: func, block: emptyClass, abort: RuntimeAbort.slabClassServesNoObject, ids: ids)

	let numChunks = emitSpanChunkCount(module, block: cut, slotSize: slotSize, totalSlots: totalSlots, ids: ids)
	let pageBase = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(cut, op: StdOp.call(pageBase, callee: SlabArenaAllocChunksName, args: argsOf(numChunks)))

	let span = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(cut, op: StdOp.call(span, callee: SlabMetaAllocName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	emitStoreField(module, block: cut, record: span, offset: MspanBaseAddrOffset, value: pageBase)
	emitStoreField(module, block: cut, record: span, offset: MspanFreeListOffset, value: none)
	emitStoreField(module, block: cut, record: span, offset: MspanFreeCountOffset, value: totalSlots)
	emitStoreField(module, block: cut, record: span, offset: MspanNextSpanOffset, value: none)
	emitStoreField(module, block: cut, record: span, offset: MspanClassIdxOffset, value: classIdx)
	emitStoreField(module, block: cut, record: span, offset: MspanBumpNextOffset, value: pageBase)

	// Every page of the run maps to this span, which is what lets a free of a pointer anywhere INSIDE it
	// find its owner (`SlabArena.buildSlabArenaMapSet`). Chunks and pages are the same 8 KiB unit.
	module.appendOp(cut, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: SlabArenaMapSetName, args: threeArgs(pageBase, second: numChunks, third: span)))
	module.setTerminator(cut, op: StdOp.branch(install.id))
	cut.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(install.id, argIds: argsOf(span)))

	let installed = ids.mint()
	install.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(installed, argType: StdType.i64))
	emitStoreField(module, block: install, record: mcacheSlot, offset: 0, value: installed)

	// ⭐⭐ **THE OWNER IS STAMPED HERE AND NOWHERE ELSE.** A span off mcentral carries `MspanOwningPParked`
	// and a freshly cut one carries whatever its RECYCLED header held, so both arms need it — and the pair
	// "installed in `row`, owned by `myP`" must be established together or a free of one of its slots routes
	// somewhere the alloc side is not looking.
	emitStoreField(module, block: install, record: installed, offset: MspanOwningPOffset, value: myP)

	// ⭐ **THE GRACE RESETS HERE, AT THE ONE PLACE BOTH PATHS MEET** — a span taken off mcentral was almost
	// certainly `SEEN_FREE` (that is what the scavenger left it as), and a span cut from a fresh chunk run is
	// in a header that may be RECYCLED and hold whatever the previous occupant's state was. One store answers
	// both, and putting it in either arm instead would leave the other arm to be remembered.
	let active = emitConst(module, block: install, value: MspanScavActive, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: install, record: installed, offset: MspanScavStateOffset, value: active)
	emitSlabLockLeave(module, block: install, state: state, sharded: sharded, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(install, op: StdOp.ret(installed))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSlabRefill'

// **POP A SLOT FROM `row`'s CACHED SPAN — the hot path**, emitted INTO the caller's blocks and answering in
// `slotId`, the returned block's single argument.
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT WAS `__slab_alloc_class(classIdx, myP, row)`, A FUNCTION, UNTIL EC8 — AND WITH IT WENT THE LAST
// FRAME ON THE HIT PATH.** One allocation used to be FIVE nested frames (`__mm_alloc` → `__slab_alloc` →
// `__slab_class_index_for` → `__slab_alloc_class` → `__slab_state_base`, plus `__slab_shard_p` on a sharded
// build); it is now two, and only the first is a call the box layer makes. What is emitted is the same ops
// in the same order — the probe, the two arms, the INV-1 trap, the consume, the eviction — so every
// argument below is unchanged. `zeroed` and `sharded` are still BUILD-time, so neither costs a branch.
//
// ⚠ **THE CALLER OWES `state`.** It is hoisted out because a SHARDED entry needs the state region's address
// BEFORE this body runs — that is where `__slab_lock` lives — so fetching it here as well would be the same
// `.data` word read twice on one path. `emitSlabStateBase` is the one spelling either caller uses.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE TWO ARMS ARE THE DESIGN.** A RECYCLED slot comes off the free list and is DIRTY, so the
// zeroing build fills it; a VIRGIN slot is carved off the bump cursor and still holds the OS's zeroes, so it
// costs nothing at any size class. That asymmetry is the whole reason the bump region exists — see the
// file header, and v1's own deletion of `__slab_build_freelist`.
//
// ⭐ **`zeroed` IS A BUILD-TIME ARGUMENT, NOT A RUNTIME `needzero` PARAMETER.** v1 threads a flag through
// `__slab_alloc_needzero` → `__slab_alloc_class`, which puts a branch on the hot path of every allocation in
// the language and an extra argument in every call. Here the two spellings are two EMISSIONS of one emitter,
// into the entry doors that differ by exactly that bit, so no program carries the arm it never takes and no
// allocation tests for it.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE MISS PATH IS TWO-PHASE IN A SHARDED BUILD, AND THE SECOND PHASE IS NOT AN OPTIMISATION.**
// A miss first DRAINS this P's remote-free queue and probes again: slots other Ps freed back to us are put
// on their spans' free lists by the drain, so a refill that skipped it would cut a fresh span while this P's
// own reclaimed memory sat unreplayed. The re-probe is also required for CORRECTNESS rather than thrift — a
// replayed slot can complete a span, and `__slab_span_return` then clears the very mcache entry the first
// probe read.
//
// ⚠ **NOTHING HERE TAKES THE LOCK.** Every word this emits belongs to a span `myP` solely owns
// (`emitSlabProbe`'s ownership gate is what establishes that) or to `row`'s own mcache entry. The one caller
// whose row is NOT solely owned — a thread on the shared raw row — is serialised by `buildSlabAllocEntry`
// before it ever gets here.
function emitSlabAllocClass(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, classIdx ValueId, myP ValueId, row ValueId, state ValueId, zeroed bool, sharded bool, slotId ValueId, ids ValueMinter, label ByteArray) returns IrBlock
	var refill = module.addBlock(func, label: label)
	var ready = module.addBlock(func, label: label)
	var recycled = module.addBlock(func, label: label)
	var virgin = module.addBlock(func, label: label)
	var handOut = module.addBlock(func, label: label)
	var pastEnd = module.addBlock(func, label: label)
	var consumed = module.addBlock(func, label: label)
	var evict = module.addBlock(func, label: label)
	var done = module.addBlock(func, label: label)
	done.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(slotId, argType: StdType.i64))

	let span = ids.mint()
	ready.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(span, argType: StdType.i64))

	let mcacheSlot = emitMcacheSlotAddr(module, block: cur, state: state, row: row, classIdx: classIdx, ids: ids)

	if sharded 'drainBeforeRefill'
		var drain = module.addBlock(func, label: label)
		var reprobe = module.addBlock(func, label: label)

		emitSlabProbe(module, func: func, cur: cur, mcacheSlot: mcacheSlot, myP: myP, sharded: sharded, hit: ready, miss: drain, ids: ids, label: label)

		module.appendOp(drain, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: SlabDrainRemoteName, args: argsOf(myP)))
		module.setTerminator(drain, op: StdOp.branch(reprobe.id))

		emitSlabProbe(module, func: func, cur: reprobe, mcacheSlot: mcacheSlot, myP: myP, sharded: sharded, hit: ready, miss: refill, ids: ids, label: label)
	end 'drainBeforeRefill' else 'refillOnMiss'
		emitSlabProbe(module, func: func, cur: cur, mcacheSlot: mcacheSlot, myP: myP, sharded: sharded, hit: ready, miss: refill, ids: ids, label: label)
	end 'refillOnMiss'

	let fresh = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(refill, op: StdOp.call(fresh, callee: SlabRefillName, args: threeArgs(classIdx, second: myP, third: row)))
	module.setTerminator(refill, op: StdOp.branch(ready.id))
	refill.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(ready.id, argIds: argsOf(fresh)))

	let none = emitConst(module, block: ready, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let geom = emitClassGeometry(module, block: ready, classIdx: classIdx, ids: ids)
	let slotSize = emitGeomSlotSize(module, block: ready, geom: geom, ids: ids)
	let freeList = emitLoadField(module, block: ready, record: span, offset: MspanFreeListOffset, ids: ids)
	let listEmpty = emitCmp(module, block: ready, a: freeList, b: none, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(ready, op: StdOp.condBranch(listEmpty, thenBlockId: virgin.id, elseBlockId: recycled.id))

	// POP. The link lives in the dead slot's first word, so unlinking is one load and one store — and the
	// fill that follows subsumes the link, which is why there is no separate first-word clear.
	let following = emitLoadField(module, block: recycled, record: freeList, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: recycled, record: span, offset: MspanFreeListOffset, value: following)
	if zeroed 'restoreTheZeroingContract'
		module.appendOp(recycled, op: StdOp.memFill(freeList, byteCountId: slotSize, value: 0))
	end 'restoreTheZeroingContract'
	module.setTerminator(recycled, op: StdOp.branch(consumed.id))
	recycled.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(consumed.id, argIds: argsOf(freeList)))

	// CARVE. INV-2 says `bump_end == base_addr + slot_size * total_slots`, derived here rather than stored;
	// INV-1 says `free_count > 0` with an empty free list implies `bump_next < bump_end`.
	let bumpNext = emitLoadField(module, block: virgin, record: span, offset: MspanBumpNextOffset, ids: ids)
	let totalSlots = emitGeomTotalSlots(module, block: virgin, geom: geom, ids: ids)
	let regionBytes = emitBin(module, block: virgin, a: slotSize, b: totalSlots, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	let baseAddr = emitLoadField(module, block: virgin, record: span, offset: MspanBaseAddrOffset, ids: ids)
	let bumpEnd = emitBin(module, block: virgin, a: baseAddr, b: regionBytes, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	// ⚠ **INV-1 TRAPS LOUDLY.** Handing out a slot past the span's end writes into whatever the arena placed
	// in the next chunk, and the corruption surfaces somewhere else, much later, as a slot handed out twice.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT IS ALSO THE TRAP THAT NAMED S5's BUG.** `alloc-torture` at `MAXON_MAX_PROCS=2` died HERE, exit
	// 86, because two OS threads shared one mcache row and popped the same span's cursor — an allocator that
	// reports its own broken invariant instead of leaving a fault somewhere downstream.
	let exhausted = emitCmp(module, block: virgin, a: bumpNext, b: bumpEnd, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(virgin, op: StdOp.condBranch(exhausted, thenBlockId: pastEnd.id, elseBlockId: handOut.id))

	emitRuntimeAbort(module, func: func, block: pastEnd, abort: RuntimeAbort.slabSpanExhaustedPastItsEnd, ids: ids)

	let nextBump = emitBin(module, block: handOut, a: bumpNext, b: slotSize, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: handOut, record: span, offset: MspanBumpNextOffset, value: nextBump)
	module.setTerminator(handOut, op: StdOp.branch(consumed.id))
	handOut.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(consumed.id, argIds: argsOf(bumpNext)))

	let slot = ids.mint()
	consumed.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(slot, argType: StdType.i64))

	// Both arms consumed exactly one slot, which is the other half of INV-1.
	let live = emitLoadField(module, block: consumed, record: span, offset: MspanFreeCountOffset, ids: ids)
	let one = emitConst(module, block: consumed, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let remaining = emitBin(module, block: consumed, a: live, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: consumed, record: span, offset: MspanFreeCountOffset, value: remaining)
	let empty = emitConst(module, block: consumed, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let drainedNow = emitCmp(module, block: consumed, a: remaining, b: empty, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(consumed, op: StdOp.condBranch(drainedNow, thenBlockId: evict.id, elseBlockId: done.id))
	consumed.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(done.id, argIds: argsOf(slot)))

	// Drop a drained span from the mcache so the next call refills cleanly.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE SPAN KEEPS ITS OWNER.** It is out of the cache but not out of `myP`'s hands: its slots are
	// still live and every one of their frees must route to this P, which is exactly what `owning_p` decides.
	// The owner is cleared once — in `__slab_span_return`, when the last slot comes back.
	let uncached = emitConst(module, block: evict, value: 0, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: evict, record: mcacheSlot, offset: 0, value: uncached)
	module.setTerminator(evict, op: StdOp.branch(done.id))
	evict.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(done.id, argIds: argsOf(slot)))

	return done
end 'emitSlabAllocClass'

// ⭐⭐ **ONE PROBE OF THE mcache ENTRY — the three questions that decide hit from miss, spelled ONCE.**
// v1 writes this out twice inside one function and its second copy's comment says so out loud (*"Same
// ownership gate as the initial check"*); two copies of a three-part predicate is two places for the third
// part to go missing, and the third part is the one that prevents heap corruption.
//
// The questions, in increasing cost:
//   1. **Is there a span at all?** The entry is 0 before the first refill and after an eviction.
//   2. **Has it any free slots?** A span whose last slot went out is evicted below, so this can only be a
//      span this row never consumed from — it is still a miss, and testing it costs one load either way.
//   3. **Do I own it?** ⭐ THE ONE THE OTHER TWO CANNOT ANSWER. A span recycled to another P keeps
//      `free_count != 0`, so question 2 reads it as a hit; popping it would mutate a free list whose sole
//      writer is somebody else. Refill instead — which is a MISS, not an error.
//
// ⚠ **THE OWNERSHIP LOAD NEEDS NO BARRIER, AND THE REASON IS ARCHITECTURAL RATHER THAN INCIDENTAL.** x64 is
// TSO, so a plain load is already acquire-ordered against the stores that published the span; arm64 and wasm
// reach this file only through an unsharded build, where the gate is not emitted at all. v1 states the same
// for its own gate.
//
// ⚠ **THIS EDGE CARRIES A BLOCK ARGUMENT OFF A CONDITIONAL, WHICH `SlabArena.maxon` DELIBERATELY NEVER
// DOES — and the divergence is deliberate, not an oversight.** That file routes every arg-carrying edge
// through an extra single-successor block, on the stated grounds that it keeps the edge safe. It is safe
// either way: `splitCriticalEdges` runs before coloring and splits exactly this shape (a pred with two
// successors into a succ with two predecessors), so the extra block is BUILT rather than written. Writing
// it by hand duplicates a pass, and a hand-written one is a second place for the edge's arguments to be
// wrong.
function emitSlabProbe(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, mcacheSlot ValueId, myP ValueId, sharded bool, hit IrBlock, miss IrBlock, ids ValueMinter, label ByteArray)
	var depleted = module.addBlock(func, label: label)

	let cached = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: mcacheSlot, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	let none = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let noSpan = emitCmp(module, block: cur, a: cached, b: none, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(noSpan, thenBlockId: miss.id, elseBlockId: depleted.id))

	let freeCount = emitLoadField(module, block: depleted, record: cached, offset: MspanFreeCountOffset, ids: ids)
	let empty = emitConst(module, block: depleted, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let drained = emitCmp(module, block: depleted, a: freeCount, b: empty, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)

	if not sharded 'noOwnerToCheck'
		module.setTerminator(depleted, op: StdOp.condBranch(drained, thenBlockId: miss.id, elseBlockId: hit.id))
		depleted.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(hit.id, argIds: argsOf(cached)))
		return
	end 'noOwnerToCheck'

	var owned = module.addBlock(func, label: label)
	module.setTerminator(depleted, op: StdOp.condBranch(drained, thenBlockId: miss.id, elseBlockId: owned.id))

	let owner = emitLoadField(module, block: owned, record: cached, offset: MspanOwningPOffset, ids: ids)
	let mine = emitCmp(module, block: owned, a: owner, b: myP, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(owned, op: StdOp.condBranch(mine, thenBlockId: hit.id, elseBlockId: miss.id))
	owned.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(hit.id, argIds: argsOf(cached)))
end 'emitSlabProbe'

// `__slab_alloc(size) -> ptr` and its two siblings — the public doors.
//
// ⭐ **`countRaw` DECIDES WHETHER THIS BODY MAINTAINS THE RAW TRAFFIC COLUMNS**, which is what lets one
// builder serve `__slab_alloc` and its uncounted twin `__slab_alloc_box`; `zeroed` decides whether a
// recycled slot is zeroed. Both are BUILD-time, so neither costs a branch or an argument at run time.
//
// The columns are credited in the entry block — before the routing, because the request is counted whichever
// tier serves it and `size` is the number either way.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE WHOLE HIT PATH IS THIS ONE BODY (EC8): the class lookup, the P, the row, the state region and
// the pop, with a call left only where the path is COLD.** What survives as a `call` is exactly what a hit
// never reaches — `__slab_os_direct_alloc` (a mapping of its own), `__slab_state_base`'s commit (once per
// process), `__slab_refill` (a fresh span) and `__slab_drain_remote` (a miss on a sharded build). Everything
// else is emitted here, so an allocation that hits its mcache entry enters ONE frame and leaves it.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THIS IS WHERE "WHICH P AM I" IS ANSWERED, ONCE PER ALLOCATION, AND HANDED DOWN.** The pop and
// `__slab_refill` both need the P and its row and neither re-derives them; v1 reads its TLS slot in the
// entry AND again in the class allocator AND clamps in both, which is one fact computed three times, with
// three chances for two of them to disagree about which row a span was stamped for.
//
// ⭐⭐ **AND WHERE THE SHARED RAW ROW IS SERIALISED.** `SlabRawShardRow` belongs to every OS thread that owns
// no P — a subprocess worker, the IOCP completion thread — and to any P clamped onto it, so the "sole writer"
// argument the lockless pop rests on does not hold there. Taking the lock around the whole class-alloc is
// what restores it. ⚠ Only ONCE A SCHEDULER EXISTS: before that the raw row belongs to the lone main thread,
// which is v1's argument (*"no raw thread ever takes the unlocked path"* — `__gt_init` runs on main before
// any worker exists) with shv2's own monotonic predicate in place of its P[0] probe.
//
// ⚠ **THE LOCK IS RECURSIVE AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING**, exactly as it is in v1: the pop under it can miss
// and call `__slab_refill`, which takes the same lock again. A non-recursive mutex here is a deadlock, not a
// performance question.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE LOCK IS TAKEN AND RELEASED AROUND ONE COPY OF THE POP, NOT AROUND TWO — `heldLock` IS WHY.**
// Serialised and sole-writer differ in exactly one thing: whether `__slab_lock` is held across the pop. With
// the pop emitted inline, spelling that as two arms would emit the body TWICE per entry door — three doors
// in a counted build, so six copies of it — for a difference of two ops. The two arms therefore converge on
// one `serve` block carrying `SlabLockTaken`/`SlabLockNotTaken`, and the release re-reads that flag. It is
// the honest form of the question too: *"did I take it"* is a fact about this call, and re-deriving it after
// the pop from `row` and the scheduler word would be asking two predicates to agree about one event.
function buildSlabAllocEntry(module StdModule, name ByteArray, countRaw bool, zeroed bool, sharded bool)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(name, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var osDirect = module.addBlock(func, label: b"osdirect")
	var small = module.addBlock(func, label: b"small")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let size = 0 as ValueId

	if countRaw 'rawColumns'
		emitRawTrafficCredit(module, block: entry, bytes: size, multiM: sharded, ids: ids)
	end 'rawColumns'

	emitSlabSizeRouting(module, block: entry, size: size, osDirect: osDirect, small: small, ids: ids)

	// ⚠ **THE OS-DIRECT TIER TAKES NO LOCK, WHERE v1's DOES — AND THAT IS A DIFFERENCE IN THE DESIGN, NOT IN
	// THE CAUTION.** v1 serialises it because its mapping length lives in a 512-entry SIDE TABLE every
	// allocation mutates. shv2 keeps the length in a 16-byte PREFIX inside the mapping itself
	// (`OsDirectPrefixBytes`), so an OS-direct alloc and free share no state with any other thread at all:
	// one `osAllocPages`, two stores into pages nobody else can name yet. A lock here would guard nothing.
	let mapped = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(osDirect, op: StdOp.call(mapped, callee: SlabOsDirectAllocName, args: argsOf(size)))
	module.setTerminator(osDirect, op: StdOp.ret(mapped))

	let class = ids.mint()
	var classReady = emitSlabClassIndex(module, func: func, cur: small, size: size, classId: class, ids: ids, label: b"cls")

	let myP = ids.mint()
	var pReady = emitSlabCurrentP(module, func: func, cur: classReady, sharded: sharded, pId: myP, ids: ids, label: b"myp")

	let row = ids.mint()
	var rowReady = emitSlabShardRow(module, func: func, cur: pReady, myP: myP, sharded: sharded, rowId: row, ids: ids, label: b"row")

	// The state region is fetched HERE rather than inside the pop because the serialised arm needs
	// `__slab_lock`'s address before the pop runs — see `emitSlabAllocClass`'s note on who owes `state`.
	let state = ids.mint()
	var stateReady = emitSlabStateBase(module, func: func, cur: rowReady, stateId: state, ids: ids, label: b"state")

	if not sharded 'noSharedRow'
		let slot = ids.mint()
		var served = emitSlabAllocClass(module, func: func, cur: stateReady, classIdx: class, myP: myP, row: row, state: state, zeroed: zeroed, sharded: sharded, slotId: slot, ids: ids, label: b"pop")
		module.setTerminator(served, op: StdOp.ret(slot))
		module.functions.push(func)
		return
	end 'noSharedRow'

	var maybeShared = module.addBlock(func, label: b"maybeshared")
	var takeLock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"serialised")
	var soleWriter = module.addBlock(func, label: b"solewriter")
	var serve = module.addBlock(func, label: b"serve")

	let heldLock = ids.mint()
	serve.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(heldLock, argType: StdType.i64))

	let sharedRow = emitConst(module, block: stateReady, value: SlabRawShardRow, ids: ids)
	let onSharedRow = emitCmp(module, block: stateReady, a: row, b: sharedRow, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(stateReady, op: StdOp.condBranch(onSharedRow, thenBlockId: maybeShared.id, elseBlockId: soleWriter.id))

	let threadsPossible = emitSlabSchedulerExists(module, block: maybeShared, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(maybeShared, op: StdOp.condBranch(threadsPossible, thenBlockId: takeLock.id, elseBlockId: soleWriter.id))

	emitSlabLockEnter(module, block: takeLock, state: state, sharded: sharded, ids: ids)
	let taken = emitConst(module, block: takeLock, value: SlabLockTaken, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(takeLock, op: StdOp.branch(serve.id))
	takeLock.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(serve.id, argIds: argsOf(taken)))

	let untaken = emitConst(module, block: soleWriter, value: SlabLockNotTaken, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(soleWriter, op: StdOp.branch(serve.id))
	soleWriter.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(serve.id, argIds: argsOf(untaken)))

	let slot = ids.mint()
	var served = emitSlabAllocClass(module, func: func, cur: serve, classIdx: class, myP: myP, row: row, state: state, zeroed: zeroed, sharded: sharded, slotId: slot, ids: ids, label: b"pop")

	var release = module.addBlock(func, label: b"release")
	var handBack = module.addBlock(func, label: b"handback")

	let notTaken = emitConst(module, block: served, value: SlabLockNotTaken, ids: ids)
	let wasSerialised = emitCmp(module, block: served, a: heldLock, b: notTaken, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(served, op: StdOp.condBranch(wasSerialised, thenBlockId: release.id, elseBlockId: handBack.id))

	emitSlabLockLeave(module, block: release, state: state, sharded: sharded, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(release, op: StdOp.ret(slot))

	module.setTerminator(handBack, op: StdOp.ret(slot))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSlabAllocEntry'

// `__slab_span_return(span)` — park a fully-emptied span on its class's mcentral list so a later refill can
// take it back.
//
// ⛔⛔ **v1 WALKS ALL 256 mcache ROWS HERE AND THAT DOES NOT PORT.** Its `__slab_mcentral_return_span` runs
// a 256-iteration load-and-compare loop NULLing any row still pointing at the span — every time a span
// empties, for a question one load answers. ⭐ **THAT LOAD IS THE OWNER'S ROW**: a span is only ever
// installed in its OWNER's mcache entry (`__slab_refill` does both in one block) and only ever reaches
// mcentral through here, so `clamp(owning_p->id)` names the one row that can possibly hold it. The compare
// against `span` is what makes the eviction exactly as complete as v1's sweep.
//
// ⚠ **THE OWNER MUST BE READ BEFORE IT IS OVERWRITTEN.** The parked stamp below is the last thing this
// function writes for that reason: reading it afterwards would clamp `MspanOwningPParked` and evict the raw
// row on every return.
//
// ⚠ **THE FREE LIST AND THE BUMP CURSOR ARE LEFT ALONE.** A parked span keeps both, and the next taker
// keeps using both — see the file header's "the case that looks wrong". Resetting either here would either
// lose recycled slots or, worse, re-virginise a region that is no longer zero and break INV-3.
//
// ⚠ **AND SO IS THE SCAVENGE GRACE STATE**, which is `ACTIVE` by the time a span gets here because
// `__slab_refill`'s install wrote it when this span was handed out. Writing it a second time here would be
// the same fact stated at two sites, and the site that would eventually go missing is this one — a span can
// reach mcentral only through the install that already answered.
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT TAKES `__slab_lock`, AND ITS CALLERS DELIBERATELY DO NOT.** The mcentral head is shared by every
// P; a span's own free list is not. Locking HERE rather than at the three `__slab_free_to_span` call sites
// keeps the common free — which never empties a span — entirely lock-free, and is why the lock has to be
// recursive: the shared-raw-row free path is already inside it when it gets here.
function buildSlabSpanReturn(module StdModule, sharded bool)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SlabSpanReturnName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var evict = module.addBlock(func, label: b"evict")
	var park = module.addBlock(func, label: b"park")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let span = 0 as ValueId

	let state = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(state, callee: SlabStateName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	emitSlabLockEnter(module, block: entry, state: state, sharded: sharded, ids: ids)
	let classIdx = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: span, offset: MspanClassIdxOffset, ids: ids)
	let mcentralSlot = emitStateSlotAddr(module, block: entry, state: state, arrayOffset: SlabStateMcentralOffset as ParsedInt, index: classIdx, ids: ids)
	let owner = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: span, offset: MspanOwningPOffset, ids: ids)

	let ownerRow = ids.mint()
	var rowReady = emitSlabShardRow(module, func: func, cur: entry, myP: owner, sharded: sharded, rowId: ownerRow, ids: ids, label: b"ownerrow")

	let mcacheSlot = emitMcacheSlotAddr(module, block: rowReady, state: state, row: ownerRow, classIdx: classIdx, ids: ids)
	let cached = emitLoadField(module, block: rowReady, record: mcacheSlot, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	let stillCached = emitCmp(module, block: rowReady, a: cached, b: span, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(rowReady, op: StdOp.condBranch(stillCached, thenBlockId: evict.id, elseBlockId: park.id))

	let uncached = emitConst(module, block: evict, value: 0, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: evict, record: mcacheSlot, offset: 0, value: uncached)
	module.setTerminator(evict, op: StdOp.branch(park.id))

	let head = emitLoadField(module, block: park, record: mcentralSlot, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: park, record: span, offset: MspanNextSpanOffset, value: head)
	emitStoreField(module, block: park, record: mcentralSlot, offset: 0, value: span)
	let parked = emitConst(module, block: park, value: MspanOwningPParked, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: park, record: span, offset: MspanOwningPOffset, value: parked)
	emitSlabLockLeave(module, block: park, state: state, sharded: sharded, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(park, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSlabSpanReturn'

// `__slab_span_destroy(span)` — take a span apart: unregister its pages, hand its chunk run back to the
// arena, and put its header on the metadata free list.
//
// ⚠ **THE CALLER MUST HAVE UNLINKED IT FROM ITS MCENTRAL LIST ALREADY, MUST HOLD `__slab_lock`, AND MUST
// HAVE ESTABLISHED THAT NO SLOT IN IT IS LIVE.** `__slab_scavenge` is the only caller and all three facts
// are structural there: a span on an mcentral list is fully free by construction (`__slab_span_return` only
// ever parks an emptied one, and `__slab_refill` removes one before handing it to a caller), the scavenger
// detaches the whole list before it walks, and it holds the lock across its entire body.
//
// ⭐ **THE REVERSE MAP IS CLEARED, AND IT IS NOT MERELY TIDINESS.** Left registered, a stale slot points at
// an mspan header that `__slab_meta_free` is about to recycle for an unrelated span at an unrelated address
// — so a `__slab_free` of a dangling pointer into the released region would push that pointer onto a LIVE
// span's free list and hand it out as a slot. Cleared, the same free takes the map's MISS route and hits
// `slabOsDirectPointerNotOurs`: a named abort instead of silent heap corruption. `__slab_arena_map_set` with
// `ArenaMapNoSpan` is the clear, because "no span owns this page" is exactly what a fresh slot says.
function buildSlabSpanDestroy(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SlabSpanDestroyName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let span = 0 as ValueId

	let classIdx = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: span, offset: MspanClassIdxOffset, ids: ids)
	let geom = emitClassGeometry(module, block: entry, classIdx: classIdx, ids: ids)
	let slotSize = emitGeomSlotSize(module, block: entry, geom: geom, ids: ids)
	let totalSlots = emitGeomTotalSlots(module, block: entry, geom: geom, ids: ids)
	let numChunks = emitSpanChunkCount(module, block: entry, slotSize: slotSize, totalSlots: totalSlots, ids: ids)
	let pageBase = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: span, offset: MspanBaseAddrOffset, ids: ids)

	let unowned = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: ArenaMapNoSpan, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: SlabArenaMapSetName, args: threeArgs(pageBase, second: numChunks, third: unowned)))

	// The arena is ASKED rather than remembered — see `SlabArena.__slab_arena_of` for why a stored field
	// would have cost the mspan record its last word to answer a question only a destruction asks.
	let arenaBase = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(arenaBase, callee: SlabArenaOfName, args: argsOf(pageBase)))
	let byteOffset = emitBin(module, block: entry, a: pageBase, b: arenaBase, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let pageShift = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: SlabPageShift, ids: ids)
	let chunkIndex = emitBin(module, block: entry, a: byteOffset, b: pageShift, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shr, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: SlabArenaFreeChunksName, args: threeArgs(arenaBase, second: chunkIndex, third: numChunks)))

	// LAST, because everything above reads the header it hands back.
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: SlabMetaFreeName, args: argsOf(span)))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSlabSpanDestroy'

// `__slab_scavenge() -> bytesDecommitted` — the whole reclamation pass, and the public door
// `__Builtins.scavengeMemory()` lowers to.
//
// **Three steps, in one call:** every idle span on every mcentral list is marked `SEEN_FREE`; every span
// that was ALREADY `SEEN_FREE` is destroyed, returning its chunks to the arena; and then
// `__slab_arena_scavenge` drops the backing of every commit granule those releases have left entirely free.
// The answer is that last step's byte count — the only number this layer can honestly report, because
// whether the pages left the RESIDENT SET is the OS's answer and not the allocator's.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE TWO-EPOCH GRACE IS THE WHOLE POINT AND IT IS WHY NOTHING COMES BACK ON THE FIRST CALL.** A span
// that empties and refills between two scavenges is reset to `ACTIVE` by `__slab_refill`'s install, so it
// never reaches the destroy arm — the active working set is never released, never memzeroed and never
// decommitted. v1 states the identical argument for the identical guard. A caller that wants memory back
// after a burst therefore calls this TWICE, which the spec says out loud.
//
// ⛔ **NO BACKGROUND SCAVENGER, AND THAT IS A PROPERTY OF THIS RUNTIME RATHER THAN A PREFERENCE.** Go's is a
// goroutine paced against the GC's heap goal at 1% of mutator time (`mgcscavenge.go`), and neither half of
// that is reachable here: shv2 has no GC and so no heap goal to pace against, its scheduler is
// cooperative with no timer to hang a background G on, and green threads exist on ONE of the five lanes
// (`GtRuntime`), so a scavenger built on them would not run in a wasm or POSIX program at all. What Go's
// design contributes and this does take is the SHAPE UNDERNEATH the pacing — release at the page layer, in
// physical-page-aligned units, gated on a run having stayed idle — which is `__slab_arena_scavenge`.
//
// ⭐ **THE LIST IS DETACHED AND REBUILT RATHER THAN SPLICED.** Removing a span from the middle of a singly
// linked list needs a `prev` carried through the walk, and a `prev` that is not updated on exactly the
// surviving arm is how a walk drops the rest of its own list. Taking the whole list, walking it and pushing
// the survivors onto a fresh head has no such variable. It reverses the order, which nothing depends on:
// mcentral is a pool, not a queue.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE WHOLE PASS RUNS UNDER `__slab_lock`, WHICH IS WHY EVERYTHING IT CALLS NEEDS NONE.** It reads and
// rewrites every mcentral head, destroys spans (returning chunks to the arena and headers to the metadata
// slab) and then walks the arena's commit bitmaps — four shared structures, and a refill racing any one of
// them could hand a P a span this pass is in the middle of taking apart.
function buildSlabScavenge(module StdModule, sharded bool)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SlabScavengeName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var classLoop = module.addBlock(func, label: b"classloop")
	var arenas = module.addBlock(func, label: b"arenas")
	var classStart = module.addBlock(func, label: b"classstart")
	var spanLoop = module.addBlock(func, label: b"spanloop")
	var spanBody = module.addBlock(func, label: b"spanbody")
	var markSeen = module.addBlock(func, label: b"markseen")
	var destroy = module.addBlock(func, label: b"destroy")
	var classDone = module.addBlock(func, label: b"classdone")

	let state = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(state, callee: SlabStateName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	emitSlabLockEnter(module, block: entry, state: state, sharded: sharded, ids: ids)
	let firstClass = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.branch(classLoop.id))
	entry.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(classLoop.id, argIds: argsOf(firstClass)))

	let classIdx = ids.mint()
	classLoop.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(classIdx, argType: StdType.i64))

	let classCount = emitConst(module, block: classLoop, value: slabClassCount(), ids: ids)
	let swept = emitCmp(module, block: classLoop, a: classIdx, b: classCount, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(classLoop, op: StdOp.condBranch(swept, thenBlockId: arenas.id, elseBlockId: classStart.id))

	let decommitted = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(arenas, op: StdOp.call(decommitted, callee: SlabArenaScavengeName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	emitSlabLockLeave(module, block: arenas, state: state, sharded: sharded, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(arenas, op: StdOp.ret(decommitted))

	let mcentralSlot = emitStateSlotAddr(module, block: classStart, state: state, arrayOffset: SlabStateMcentralOffset as ParsedInt, index: classIdx, ids: ids)
	let head = emitLoadField(module, block: classStart, record: mcentralSlot, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	let empty = emitConst(module, block: classStart, value: 0, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: classStart, record: mcentralSlot, offset: 0, value: empty)
	module.setTerminator(classStart, op: StdOp.branch(spanLoop.id))
	classStart.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(spanLoop.id, argIds: twoArgs(head, second: empty)))

	// The span under the walk, and the head of the list being rebuilt out of the ones that survive it.
	let span = ids.mint()
	let kept = ids.mint()
	spanLoop.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(span, argType: StdType.i64))
	spanLoop.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(kept, argType: StdType.i64))

	let none = emitConst(module, block: spanLoop, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let walked = emitCmp(module, block: spanLoop, a: span, b: none, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(spanLoop, op: StdOp.condBranch(walked, thenBlockId: classDone.id, elseBlockId: spanBody.id))
	spanLoop.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(classDone.id, argIds: argsOf(kept)))

	// The successor is read BEFORE either arm, because the destroy arm hands the header to the metadata free
	// list, which writes its own link over the span's first word — and a walk that read `next` afterwards
	// would follow the free list instead of the mcentral one.
	let following = emitLoadField(module, block: spanBody, record: span, offset: MspanNextSpanOffset, ids: ids)
	let scavState = emitLoadField(module, block: spanBody, record: span, offset: MspanScavStateOffset, ids: ids)
	let active = emitConst(module, block: spanBody, value: MspanScavActive, ids: ids)
	let firstSeen = emitCmp(module, block: spanBody, a: scavState, b: active, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(spanBody, op: StdOp.condBranch(firstSeen, thenBlockId: markSeen.id, elseBlockId: destroy.id))

	let seenFree = emitConst(module, block: markSeen, value: MspanScavSeenFree, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: markSeen, record: span, offset: MspanScavStateOffset, value: seenFree)
	emitStoreField(module, block: markSeen, record: span, offset: MspanNextSpanOffset, value: kept)
	module.setTerminator(markSeen, op: StdOp.branch(spanLoop.id))
	markSeen.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(spanLoop.id, argIds: twoArgs(following, second: span)))

	module.appendOp(destroy, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: SlabSpanDestroyName, args: argsOf(span)))
	module.setTerminator(destroy, op: StdOp.branch(spanLoop.id))
	destroy.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(spanLoop.id, argIds: twoArgs(following, second: kept)))

	let survivors = ids.mint()
	classDone.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(survivors, argType: StdType.i64))
	emitStoreField(module, block: classDone, record: mcentralSlot, offset: 0, value: survivors)
	let one = emitConst(module, block: classDone, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let nextClass = emitBin(module, block: classDone, a: classIdx, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(classDone, op: StdOp.branch(classLoop.id))
	classDone.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(classLoop.id, argIds: argsOf(nextClass)))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSlabScavenge'

// `__slab_free_to_span(slot, span)` — put one slot back on its span's free list, and hand the span to
// mcentral if that was its last live one.
//
// **PUSH: `[slot] = free_list; free_list = slot; free_count += 1`** — the other half of INV-1.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE COMMON SINK FOR EVERY FREE THAT REACHES A SPAN, AND IT IS ONE EMITTER BECAUSE IT HAS THREE
// CALLERS THAT CANNOT SHARE A BLOCK.** `__slab_free`'s own local push, its serialised push for a slot on the
// shared raw row (which must run inside the lock), and the remote-queue replay in `__slab_drain_remote`;
// spelling the sequence at each would be v1's own duplication with better manners. v1 factors it
// identically and for the identical reason (`__slab_free_class_with_mspan`).
//
// ⭐ **THE HOT CALLER EMITS IT INLINE AND THE COLD ONES STILL CALL `__slab_free_to_span` (EC8).** A local
// push is ~12 ops — less than the frame that reached it — and it is the arm every free of a program with
// one thread takes. The two cold arms (a slot on the shared raw row, which runs under `__slab_lock`, and
// the drain's replay loop, which runs the push once per queued slot) keep the call, so the function still
// exists and this is one spelling emitted twice, not two spellings.
//
// ⚠⚠ **EVERY CALLER MUST BE THE SPAN'S SOLE WRITER, AND EACH ESTABLISHES THAT DIFFERENTLY** — that is the
// contract this cannot check and its callers exist to satisfy. `__slab_free`'s local arm has compared
// `owning_p` against its own P; its raw arm holds `__slab_lock`; the drain runs on the owner by
// construction, because a remote push targets the owner's queue and nobody else's.
function emitSlabFreeToSpan(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, slot ValueId, span ValueId, ids ValueMinter, label ByteArray) returns IrBlock
	var fullyFree = module.addBlock(func, label: label)
	let done = module.addBlock(func, label: label)

	let head = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: span, offset: MspanFreeListOffset, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: cur, record: slot, offset: 0, value: head)
	emitStoreField(module, block: cur, record: span, offset: MspanFreeListOffset, value: slot)
	let live = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: span, offset: MspanFreeCountOffset, ids: ids)
	let one = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let released = emitBin(module, block: cur, a: live, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: cur, record: span, offset: MspanFreeCountOffset, value: released)

	let classIdx = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: span, offset: MspanClassIdxOffset, ids: ids)
	let geom = emitClassGeometry(module, block: cur, classIdx: classIdx, ids: ids)
	let totalSlots = emitGeomTotalSlots(module, block: cur, geom: geom, ids: ids)
	let spanIsEmpty = emitCmp(module, block: cur, a: released, b: totalSlots, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(spanIsEmpty, thenBlockId: fullyFree.id, elseBlockId: done.id))

	module.appendOp(fullyFree, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: SlabSpanReturnName, args: argsOf(span)))
	module.setTerminator(fullyFree, op: StdOp.branch(done.id))

	return done
end 'emitSlabFreeToSpan'

// `__slab_free_to_span(slot, span)` — the same push behind a frame, for the two COLD arms: the serialised
// one (a slot on the shared raw row, freed once a scheduler exists) and `__slab_drain_remote`'s replay
// loop. Neither is on any program's hot path, so the frame costs them nothing they would notice and saves
// the push being written into the emitted image two more times.
function buildSlabFreeToSpan(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SlabFreeToSpanName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let slot = 0 as ValueId
	let span = 1 as ValueId

	var done = emitSlabFreeToSpan(module, func: func, cur: entry, slot: slot, span: span, ids: ids, label: b"push")
	module.setTerminator(done, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSlabFreeToSpan'

// `__slab_drain_remote(p)` — replay every slot other Ps have CAS-pushed onto `p`'s queue back onto the spans
// that own them.
//
// ⭐⭐ **ONE CAS DETACHES THE WHOLE CHAIN.** Swapping the head for 0 gives this P private ownership of every
// node at once, so the walk that follows touches nothing another thread can see — which is what lets the
// per-span pushes be the ordinary single-writer ones. Popping node by node would need a CAS per slot and
// would reintroduce the ABA problem the whole-chain detach does not have.
//
// ⚠ **`next` IS READ BEFORE THE SPAN PUSH, because that push writes the free-list link over the node's first
// word** — the same word the queue's link lives in. A walk that read it afterwards would follow the span's
// free list out of its own chain.
//
// ⚠ **CALLED ONLY BY THE OWNER, on its alloc slow path.** That is what makes it a single-CONSUMER queue and
// the per-span pushes lock-free. A `p` of `MspanOwningPNone` — an OS thread with no P — has no queue and
// returns at once; the caller does not test for it, because the test belongs where the fact is.
//
// ⚠ **A MAP MISS HERE IS CORRUPTION, NOT A ROUTE.** `__slab_free` sends map-misses to the OS-direct tier
// before anything can reach a queue, and a span is never unregistered while one of its slots is live — so a
// node whose page maps to no span means something has already gone wrong, and pushing onto a span pointer of
// `ArenaMapNoSpan` would store through address 0. v1 asserts the same invariant in a comment and does not
// check it.
function buildSlabDrainRemote(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SlabDrainRemoteName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var detach = module.addBlock(func, label: b"detach")
	var swap = module.addBlock(func, label: b"swap")
	var walk = module.addBlock(func, label: b"walk")
	var replay = module.addBlock(func, label: b"replay")
	var orphan = module.addBlock(func, label: b"orphan")
	var push = module.addBlock(func, label: b"push")
	var done = module.addBlock(func, label: b"done")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let p = 0 as ValueId

	let noProc = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: MspanOwningPNone, ids: ids)
	let hasNoQueue = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: p, b: noProc, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	let offset = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: POffRemoteFreeHead as ParsedInt, ids: ids)
	let headAddr = emitBin(module, block: entry, a: p, b: offset, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(hasNoQueue, thenBlockId: done.id, elseBlockId: detach.id))

	// ⚠ **THE EMPTINESS TEST COMES BEFORE THE CAS, AND THAT IS WHAT KEEPS THE COMMON CASE FREE.** This runs
	// on EVERY alloc miss, and on a program with no cross-P traffic the queue is empty every single time; a
	// CAS of 0-for-0 would be correct but would put an uncontended `lock cmpxchg` on every refill for a
	// chain that is not there. `detach` is the retry target, so a lost race re-reads rather than re-using a
	// head another producer has already replaced.
	let head = emitLoadField(module, block: detach, record: headAddr, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	let emptyQueue = emitConst(module, block: detach, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let nothingQueued = emitCmp(module, block: detach, a: head, b: emptyQueue, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(detach, op: StdOp.condBranch(nothingQueued, thenBlockId: done.id, elseBlockId: swap.id))

	let won = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(swap, op: StdOp.atomicCas(won, addrId: headAddr, expectedId: head, replacementId: emptyQueue))
	let lostRace = emitConst(module, block: swap, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let contended = emitCmp(module, block: swap, a: won, b: lostRace, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(swap, op: StdOp.condBranch(contended, thenBlockId: detach.id, elseBlockId: walk.id))
	swap.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(walk.id, argIds: argsOf(head)))

	let node = ids.mint()
	walk.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(node, argType: StdType.i64))
	let endOfChain = emitConst(module, block: walk, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let walked = emitCmp(module, block: walk, a: node, b: endOfChain, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(walk, op: StdOp.condBranch(walked, thenBlockId: done.id, elseBlockId: replay.id))

	let following = emitLoadField(module, block: replay, record: node, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	let span = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(replay, op: StdOp.call(span, callee: SlabArenaMapGetName, args: argsOf(node)))
	let unmapped = emitConst(module, block: replay, value: ArenaMapNoSpan, ids: ids)
	let noOwner = emitCmp(module, block: replay, a: span, b: unmapped, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(replay, op: StdOp.condBranch(noOwner, thenBlockId: orphan.id, elseBlockId: push.id))

	emitRuntimeAbort(module, func: func, block: orphan, abort: RuntimeAbort.slabRemoteFreeSlotHasNoSpan, ids: ids)

	module.appendOp(push, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: SlabFreeToSpanName, args: twoArgs(node, second: span)))
	module.setTerminator(push, op: StdOp.branch(walk.id))
	push.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(walk.id, argIds: argsOf(following)))

	module.setTerminator(done, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSlabDrainRemote'

// `__slab_free(ptr)` — return a slot to its span, to its owner's remote queue, or a mapping to the OS.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE FIRST QUESTION IS ONE: DOES THE REVERSE MAP KNOW THIS PAGE?** A hit names the owning span; a
// MISS is the OS-direct sentinel (`SlabArena.ArenaMapNoSpan`), which is why nothing may ever register an
// OS-direct mapping's pages in that map.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE SECOND IS WHO OWNS THE SPAN, AND IT HAS FOUR ANSWERS — v1's routing, ported exactly**
// (`stdlib/Internals.maxon:1279-1348`), because every arm of it is a fact about which thread may write the
// span's free list rather than a preference:
//
//   * **`MspanOwningPNone` — the shared raw row.** Any P-less OS thread may hold slots of this span and any
//     thread at all may free one, so the push is serialised under `__slab_lock` — but ONLY once a scheduler
//     exists. Before that the lone main thread is the only writer and the lock would guard nothing.
//   * **`MspanOwningPParked` — the span is on an mcentral list.** ⛔ Every one of its slots is already free,
//     so a free that reaches here is a DOUBLE FREE or a slot that outlived its span: it aborts.
//   * **the P running me.** The ordinary case: a lock-free push onto a list I am the sole writer of.
//   * **another P.** CAS-push onto that P's queue and leave; the owner replays it.
//
// ⭐ **shv2's PARKED ARM ABORTS FOR EVERY CALLER WHERE v1 ABORTS ONLY FOR A REAL P.** v1's raw-thread path
// falls through to `__slab_proc_at(0xFFFFFFFF)` — a load 32 GiB past its P array — and then to a fallback
// local push into a span sitting on an mcentral list. The counting argument is the same for both callers, so
// there is no reason for two answers: a slot in flight on a remote queue has NOT been counted into
// `free_count`, so a span cannot reach `total_slots` while one of its slots is unaccounted for, so the
// sentinel is unreachable for anybody who holds a live slot.
//
// ⚠ **A NULL TARGET CANNOT HAPPEN AND IS NOT GUARDED, WHICH IS THE OTHER HALF OF STORING A POINTER.** v1
// guards it (*"a NULL target would drop the free"*) because it reconstructs the P from an id and an array
// that may not be populated; here the pointer IS the one a live P stamped, so a non-`None`, non-`Parked`
// owner is a P that exists.
//
// ⚠ **`ptr` MUST BE A SLOT BASE, and nothing here can check it.** The free list is threaded through the
// slot's first word, so an INTERIOR pointer would corrupt the slot it lands in and then be handed out as a
// slot base. Every caller passes back exactly what `__slab_alloc` answered (`__mm_free` passes the box
// HEADER base, which is that pointer); v1 has the same obligation and states it the same way.
function buildSlabFree(module StdModule, sharded bool)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SlabFreeName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var osDirect = module.addBlock(func, label: b"osdirect")
	var toSpan = module.addBlock(func, label: b"tospan")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let ptr = 0 as ValueId

	let span = ids.mint()
	var mapped = emitSlabArenaMapGet(module, func: func, cur: entry, ptr: ptr, spanId: span, ids: ids, label: b"map")
	let noSpan = emitConst(module, block: mapped, value: ArenaMapNoSpan, ids: ids)
	let unmapped = emitCmp(module, block: mapped, a: span, b: noSpan, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(mapped, op: StdOp.condBranch(unmapped, thenBlockId: osDirect.id, elseBlockId: toSpan.id))

	// ⚠ **NO LOCK, WHERE v1 TAKES ONE** — see `buildSlabAllocEntry`'s note: shv2's OS-direct tier keeps its
	// length in the mapping's own prefix and shares no state with any other thread.
	module.appendOp(osDirect, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: SlabOsDirectFreeName, args: argsOf(ptr)))
	module.setTerminator(osDirect, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	if not sharded 'soleThread'
		var pushed = emitSlabFreeToSpan(module, func: func, cur: toSpan, slot: ptr, span: span, ids: ids, label: b"push")
		module.setTerminator(pushed, op: StdOp.retVoid)
		module.functions.push(func)
		return
	end 'soleThread'

	var rawOwned = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rawowned")
	var rawSerialised = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rawlocked")
	var notRaw = module.addBlock(func, label: b"notraw")
	var parked = module.addBlock(func, label: b"parked")
	var mine = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mine")
	var local = module.addBlock(func, label: b"local")
	var remote = module.addBlock(func, label: b"remote")

	let owner = emitLoadField(module, block: toSpan, record: span, offset: MspanOwningPOffset, ids: ids)
	let noProc = emitConst(module, block: toSpan, value: MspanOwningPNone, ids: ids)
	let onRawRow = emitCmp(module, block: toSpan, a: owner, b: noProc, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(toSpan, op: StdOp.condBranch(onRawRow, thenBlockId: rawOwned.id, elseBlockId: notRaw.id))

	let threadsPossible = emitSlabSchedulerExists(module, block: rawOwned, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(rawOwned, op: StdOp.condBranch(threadsPossible, thenBlockId: rawSerialised.id, elseBlockId: local.id))

	let state = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(rawSerialised, op: StdOp.call(state, callee: SlabStateName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	emitSlabLockEnter(module, block: rawSerialised, state: state, sharded: sharded, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(rawSerialised, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: SlabFreeToSpanName, args: twoArgs(ptr, second: span)))
	emitSlabLockLeave(module, block: rawSerialised, state: state, sharded: sharded, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(rawSerialised, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	let parkedOwner = emitConst(module, block: notRaw, value: MspanOwningPParked, ids: ids)
	let alreadyReturned = emitCmp(module, block: notRaw, a: owner, b: parkedOwner, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(notRaw, op: StdOp.condBranch(alreadyReturned, thenBlockId: parked.id, elseBlockId: mine.id))

	emitRuntimeAbort(module, func: func, block: parked, abort: RuntimeAbort.slabFreeOfParkedSpan, ids: ids)

	let myP = ids.mint()
	var pKnown = emitSlabCurrentP(module, func: func, cur: mine, sharded: sharded, pId: myP, ids: ids, label: b"myp")
	let isMine = emitCmp(module, block: pKnown, a: owner, b: myP, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(pKnown, op: StdOp.condBranch(isMine, thenBlockId: local.id, elseBlockId: remote.id))

	var locallyPushed = emitSlabFreeToSpan(module, func: func, cur: local, slot: ptr, span: span, ids: ids, label: b"push")
	module.setTerminator(locallyPushed, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	var pushed = emitSlabRemotePush(module, func: func, cur: remote, target: owner, slot: ptr, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(pushed, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSlabFree'

// `__slab_os_direct_alloc(size) -> ptr` — one mapping for one request, with its length in a prefix.
//
// See `OsDirectPrefixBytes` for why the length rides in the mapping rather than in a side table, and what
// v1's side table costs.
function buildSlabOsDirectAlloc(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SlabOsDirectAllocName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let size = 0 as ValueId

	let prefix = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: OsDirectPrefixBytes, ids: ids)
	let withPrefix = emitBin(module, block: entry, a: size, b: prefix, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let total = emitAlignUp(module, block: entry, value: withPrefix, width: SlabPageBytes, ids: ids)

	let raw = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.osAllocPages(raw, sizeId: total))
	let base = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(base, callee: SlabAssertOsAllocName, args: argsOf(raw)))

	emitStoreField(module, block: entry, record: base, offset: OsDirectTotalBytesOffset, value: total)
	let magic = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: OsDirectMagic, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: entry, record: base, offset: OsDirectMagicOffset, value: magic)

	// The pages are fresh from the OS, so the payload past the prefix is already zero — the zeroing contract
	// costs this tier nothing at all, and an OS-direct mapping is never recycled through us.
	let payload = emitBin(module, block: entry, a: base, b: prefix, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.ret(payload))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSlabOsDirectAlloc'

// `__slab_os_direct_free(ptr)` — hand one mapping back whole.
//
// ⭐ The magic word is what v1 gets out of failing to find the pointer in its tracking array (its sentinel
// exit 135): without it, a double free or a pointer that was never ours reads user data as a byte count and
// unmaps an arbitrary region.
function buildSlabOsDirectFree(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SlabOsDirectFreeName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var notOurs = module.addBlock(func, label: b"notours")
	var release = module.addBlock(func, label: b"release")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let ptr = 0 as ValueId

	let prefix = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: OsDirectPrefixBytes, ids: ids)
	let base = emitBin(module, block: entry, a: ptr, b: prefix, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let stamped = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: base, offset: OsDirectMagicOffset, ids: ids)
	let magic = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: OsDirectMagic, ids: ids)
	let ours = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: stamped, b: magic, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(ours, thenBlockId: release.id, elseBlockId: notOurs.id))

	emitRuntimeAbort(module, func: func, block: notOurs, abort: RuntimeAbort.slabOsDirectPointerNotOurs, ids: ids)

	let total = emitLoadField(module, block: release, record: base, offset: OsDirectTotalBytesOffset, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(release, op: StdOp.osFreePages(base, sizeId: total))
	module.setTerminator(release, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSlabOsDirectFree'
